window.PYQ_DATA.add({"id":"px-ncert12-phy-ray-optics","kind":"practice","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"title":"Ray Optics · Practice","chapter":"Ray Optics","subject":"Physics","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Physics, Ch 9 · Ray Optics and Optical Instruments","marking":{"mcq":[4,-1]},"rules":{},"note":"","questions":[{"id":"NCERT12-PHY-RAY-OPTICS-001","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Ray Optics","qno":1,"subject":"Physics","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Ray Optics","type":"mcq","question":"Match the object position in List-I with the nature of the image in List-II.\nList-I\n(a) Object between pole and focus of a concave mirror\n(b) Object anywhere in front of a convex mirror\n(c) Object between F and C of a concave mirror\n(d) Object at infinity in front of a concave mirror\nList-II\n(i) Real, inverted, magnified, beyond C\n(ii) Virtual, erect, diminished, between P and F behind the mirror\n(iii) Real, inverted, highly diminished, at F\n(iv) Virtual, erect, magnified, behind the mirror","image":"","options":["(a)-(ii), (b)-(iv), (c)-(i), (d)-(iii)","(a)-(iv), (b)-(ii), (c)-(iii), (d)-(i)","(a)-(iv), (b)-(ii), (c)-(i), (d)-(iii)","(a)-(i), (b)-(ii), (c)-(iv), (d)-(iii)"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["3"],"explanation":"(a) Concave mirror, object between P and F: the image is virtual, erect and magnified, behind the mirror, which is (iv). Example 9.3(ii) gives $m = +3$.\n(b) Convex mirror: the image is always virtual, erect and diminished, between P and F, which is (ii).\n(c) Object between F and C: the image is real, inverted and magnified, beyond C, which is (i).\n(d) Object at infinity: parallel rays converge at F, giving a point-sized real image, which is (iii).\nAnswer: (3)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Physics, Ch 9 · Ray Optics and Optical Instruments","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT12-PHY-RAY-OPTICS-002","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Ray Optics","qno":2,"subject":"Physics","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Ray Optics","type":"mcq","question":"Assertion (A): Turpentine is optically denser than water, although the mass density of turpentine is less than that of water.\nReason (R): The optical density of a medium is decided by its mass per unit volume.","image":"","options":["Both A and R are true and R is the correct explanation of A","Both A and R are true but R is not the correct explanation of A","A is true but R is false","A is false but R is true"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["3"],"explanation":"A is true. NCERT gives turpentine and water as the example. Turpentine has a lower mass density than water, but it has a higher optical density.\nR is false. Optical density is not mass per unit volume. It depends on the ratio of the speeds of light in the two media.\nIn fact, A only makes sense because the two kinds of density are different quantities.\nAnswer: (3)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Physics, Ch 9 · Ray Optics and Optical Instruments","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT12-PHY-RAY-OPTICS-003","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Ray Optics","qno":3,"subject":"Physics","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Ray Optics","type":"mcq","question":"Turpentine has a lower mass density than water, yet a ray of light going from water into turpentine bends towards the normal. Which statement correctly explains this?","image":"","options":["Turpentine is optically denser than water, and optical density is not the same as mass density","Light always bends towards the normal when it enters a liquid of lower mass density","Turpentine has a lower refractive index than water","Bending depends only on the angle of incidence, not on the media"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"A ray bends towards the normal when it enters an optically denser medium, that is, one with a higher refractive index.\nNCERT notes that turpentine has a lower mass density than water but a higher optical density.\nOptical density is related to the speed of light in the medium, not to mass per unit volume.\nOption (3) is wrong: a lower refractive index would make the ray bend away from the normal.\nAnswer: (1)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Physics, Ch 9 · Ray Optics and Optical Instruments","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT12-PHY-RAY-OPTICS-004","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Ray Optics","qno":4,"subject":"Physics","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Ray Optics","type":"mcq","question":"In a compound microscope, the image formed by the objective lens is","image":"","options":["virtual, erect and magnified","real, inverted and magnified","real, erect and diminished","virtual, inverted and diminished"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["2"],"explanation":"The objective is the lens nearest the object. It forms a real, inverted, magnified image of the object.\nThis image lies at or within the focal plane of the eyepiece and acts as the object for the eyepiece.\nThe eyepiece then forms the final enlarged virtual image.\nOption (1) describes the final image formed by the eyepiece, not the first image.\nAnswer: (2)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Physics, Ch 9 · Ray Optics and Optical Instruments","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT12-PHY-RAY-OPTICS-005","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Ray Optics","qno":5,"subject":"Physics","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Ray Optics","type":"mcq","question":"Statement I: A convex mirror always forms a virtual image of a real object, wherever the object is placed.\nStatement II: For a convex mirror f is positive and u is negative, so $v = \\frac{fu}{u-f}$ always comes out positive.\nChoose the correct option.","image":"","options":["Both Statement I and Statement II are correct","Both Statement I and Statement II are incorrect","Statement I is correct but Statement II is incorrect","Statement I is incorrect but Statement II is correct"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"Statement II: with f > 0 and u < 0, the numerator fu is negative. The denominator (u − f) is also negative. So v is positive. Correct.\nStatement I: a positive v means the image lies behind the mirror. Such an image is virtual, whatever the object distance. Correct.\nBoth statements are therefore true.\nAnswer: (1)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Physics, Ch 9 · Ray Optics and Optical Instruments","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT12-PHY-RAY-OPTICS-006","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Ray Optics","qno":6,"subject":"Physics","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Ray Optics","type":"mcq","question":"Electromagnetic radiation that the human eye can detect is called light. According to NCERT, its wavelength range is about:","image":"","options":["10 nm to 400 nm","400 nm to 750 nm","750 nm to 1 mm","1 nm to 10 nm"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["2"],"explanation":"The retina responds only to a narrow band of the electromagnetic spectrum.\nNCERT gives this visible band as roughly 400 nm (violet end) to 750 nm (red end).\n10 nm to 400 nm is ultraviolet, and 750 nm to 1 mm is infrared. The eye cannot detect either.\nAnswer: (2)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Physics, Ch 9 · Ray Optics and Optical Instruments","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT12-PHY-RAY-OPTICS-007","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Ray Optics","qno":7,"subject":"Physics","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Ray Optics","type":"mcq","question":"Which of the following is the main requirement in making optical fibres that carry signals over long distances?","image":"","options":["The fibre material must have a very high mass density","The fibre must be coated outside with a silver layer","The fibre material must absorb very little light","The fibre must be perfectly straight"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["3"],"explanation":"NCERT states that the main requirement is very little absorption of light over long distances. This has been achieved by purifying and specially preparing materials such as quartz.\nIn silica glass fibres, more than 95% of the light can be transmitted over 1 km of fibre.\nOption (4) is wrong because fibres guide light even when bent. Option (2) is wrong because guiding comes from TIR, not from metallic reflection.\nAnswer: (3)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Physics, Ch 9 · Ray Optics and Optical Instruments","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT12-PHY-RAY-OPTICS-008","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Ray Optics","qno":8,"subject":"Physics","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Ray Optics","type":"mcq","question":"In a compound microscope, the objective forms an intermediate image of a tiny object, and the eyepiece then forms the final image. Relative to the original object, the final image is:","image":"","options":["virtual, erect and enlarged","virtual, inverted and enlarged","real, inverted and enlarged","real, erect and diminished"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["2"],"explanation":"The objective forms a real, inverted, magnified image of the object.\nThis image lies at or within the focal plane of the eyepiece. The eyepiece acts as a simple magnifier, giving a virtual, enlarged image without inverting it again.\nSo the final image is virtual and enlarged, but inverted with respect to the object.\n'Virtual, erect' is tempting because a simple magnifier gives an erect image. Here, however, the eyepiece's object is already inverted.\nAnswer: (2)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Physics, Ch 9 · Ray Optics and Optical Instruments","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT12-PHY-RAY-OPTICS-009","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Ray Optics","qno":9,"subject":"Physics","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Ray Optics","type":"mcq","question":"A thin convex lens of focal length 20 cm is placed in contact with a thin concave lens of focal length 60 cm. An object is placed 60 cm in front of the combination. How far from the combination is the image formed?","image":"","options":["60 cm","30 cm","15 cm","20 cm"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"Given: $f_1$ = +20 cm, $f_2$ = −60 cm, u = −60 cm.\nFormula: $\\frac{1}{f} = \\frac{1}{f_1} + \\frac{1}{f_2}$. This gives $\\frac{1}{f} = \\frac{1}{20} - \\frac{1}{60} = \\frac{1}{30}$, so f = +30 cm.\nLens formula: $\\frac{1}{v} = \\frac{1}{f} + \\frac{1}{u} = \\frac{1}{30} - \\frac{1}{60} = \\frac{1}{60}$.\nSo v = +60 cm. The image is real and forms on the other side of the combination.\nAnswer: (1)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Physics, Ch 9 · Ray Optics and Optical Instruments","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT12-PHY-RAY-OPTICS-010","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Ray Optics","qno":10,"subject":"Physics","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Ray Optics","type":"mcq","question":"An equilateral glass prism ($A = 60^\\circ$) placed in air gives a minimum deviation of $30^\\circ$. What is the refractive index of the glass?","image":"","options":["1.414","1.500","1.732","1.225"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"Given: $A = 60^\\circ$, $D_m = 30^\\circ$.\nFormula: $n = \\frac{\\sin[(A + D_m)/2]}{\\sin(A/2)}$.\nSubstituting: $n = \\frac{\\sin 45^\\circ}{\\sin 30^\\circ} = \\frac{0.707}{0.5}$.\nSo $n = \\sqrt{2} \\approx 1.414$.\nAnswer: (1)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Physics, Ch 9 · Ray Optics and Optical Instruments","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT12-PHY-RAY-OPTICS-011","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Ray Optics","qno":11,"subject":"Physics","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Ray Optics","type":"mcq","question":"Statement I: A glass convex lens (n = 1.5) has a smaller power when immersed in water (n = 1.33) than when it is in air.\nStatement II: A glass lens of refractive index 1.47 immersed in a liquid of refractive index 1.47 has zero power.","image":"","options":["Both Statement I and Statement II are true","Both Statement I and Statement II are false","Statement I is true but Statement II is false","Statement I is false but Statement II is true"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"Lens maker's formula: $\\frac{1}{f} = \\frac{n_2 - n_1}{n_1}\\left(\\frac{1}{R_1} - \\frac{1}{R_2}\\right)$.\nStatement I is true. In air the factor is 0.5, while in water it is 0.17/1.33 ≈ 0.128. So the power falls, and f grows from 20 cm to about 78 cm for the NCERT example.\nStatement II is true. When $n_2 = n_1$, we get 1/f = 0, so P = 0 and the lens behaves like a plane glass sheet. This is how the lens 'disappears' in the magician example.\nAnswer: (1)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Physics, Ch 9 · Ray Optics and Optical Instruments","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT12-PHY-RAY-OPTICS-012","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Ray Optics","qno":12,"subject":"Physics","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Ray Optics","type":"mcq","question":"A beam of light is converging towards a point P. A thin concave lens of focal length 30 cm is placed in the path of the beam, 15 cm before P. At what distance from the lens do the rays now meet?","image":"","options":["30 cm","10 cm","60 cm","7.5 cm"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"Given: P acts as a virtual object on the outgoing side, so $u = +15$ cm. For the concave lens, $f = -30$ cm.\nFormula: $\\frac{1}{v} - \\frac{1}{u} = \\frac{1}{f}$\nSubstituting: $\\frac{1}{v} = -\\frac{1}{30} + \\frac{1}{15} = \\frac{1}{30}$\nSo $v$ = +30 cm. The rays now meet 30 cm beyond the lens. This is farther than P because the lens diverges the beam.\nAnswer: (1)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Physics, Ch 9 · Ray Optics and Optical Instruments","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT12-PHY-RAY-OPTICS-013","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Ray Optics","qno":13,"subject":"Physics","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Ray Optics","type":"mcq","question":"Consider the derivation of the relation for refraction at a convex spherical surface (Fig. 9.15). A point object O sits in medium $n_1$, and its real image I forms in medium $n_2$. The centre of curvature C lies on the side of the refracted light. The magnitudes OM, MI and MC are converted using the Cartesian sign convention. Which set of substitutions is correct?","image":"","options":["OM = $+u$, MI = $+v$, MC = $-R$","OM = $-u$, MI = $-v$, MC = $-R$","OM = $-u$, MI = $+v$, MC = $+R$","OM = $+u$, MI = $-v$, MC = $+R$"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["3"],"explanation":"All distances are measured from the pole M of the surface. Distances along the incident light are positive.\nThe object O lies against the incident light, so the signed distance u is negative and OM = $-u$.\nThe image I and the centre C lie along the incident light, so MI = $+v$ and MC = $+R$.\nSubstituting these into $\\frac{n_1}{OM}+\\frac{n_2}{MI}=\\frac{n_2-n_1}{MC}$ gives $\\frac{n_2}{v}-\\frac{n_1}{u}=\\frac{n_2-n_1}{R}$.\nOption (1) is wrong because it treats u as positive and R as negative.\nAnswer: (3)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Physics, Ch 9 · Ray Optics and Optical Instruments","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT12-PHY-RAY-OPTICS-014","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Ray Optics","qno":14,"subject":"Physics","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Ray Optics","type":"mcq","question":"A thin glass lens ($n = 1.5$) has a focal length of $+20$ cm in air. It is completely immersed in a transparent liquid of refractive index 1.25. What is its focal length in the liquid?","image":"","options":["$+50$ cm","$+8$ cm","$+25$ cm","$-40$ cm"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"Given: $n_g = 1.5$, $f_{air} = 20$ cm, $n_l = 1.25$.\nFormula: $\\frac{1}{f} = \\left(\\frac{n_2}{n_1} - 1\\right)\\left(\\frac{1}{R_1} - \\frac{1}{R_2}\\right)$. The radius term stays the same, so $\\frac{f_l}{f_{air}} = \\frac{0.5}{\\frac{1.5}{1.25} - 1}$.\nSubstituting: $\\frac{1.5}{1.25} - 1 = 0.2$, so $f_l = 20 \\times \\frac{0.5}{0.2}$.\nSo $f_l = +50$ cm. The lens is still converging but weaker.\nAnswer: (1)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Physics, Ch 9 · Ray Optics and Optical Instruments","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT12-PHY-RAY-OPTICS-015","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Ray Optics","qno":15,"subject":"Physics","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Ray Optics","type":"mcq","question":"A compound microscope is to give a total magnifying power of 30 with the final image at the near point (D = 25 cm). It uses an objective of focal length 1.0 cm and an eyepiece of focal length 5.0 cm. Treat the lenses as thin. What should be the separation between the objective and the eyepiece?","image":"","options":["12.0 cm","7.2 cm","10.2 cm","9.0 cm"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["3"],"explanation":"Given: M = 30, $f_o$ = 1 cm, $f_e$ = 5 cm, final image at 25 cm.\nEyepiece: $m_e = 1 + \\frac{25}{5} = 6$, so the objective must give $|m_o| = \\frac{30}{6} = 5$.\nObjective: v = 5|u|, so $\\frac{1}{5|u|} + \\frac{1}{|u|} = 1$, giving |u| = 1.2 cm and v = 6 cm.\nEyepiece: $\\frac{1}{-25} - \\frac{1}{u_e} = \\frac{1}{5}$, so $|u_e| = \\frac{25}{6} \\approx 4.17$ cm.\nSo the separation = 6 + 4.17 ≈ 10.2 cm. The value 12.0 cm wrongly assumes the final image is at infinity.\nAnswer: (3)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Physics, Ch 9 · Ray Optics and Optical Instruments","difficulty":"hard"},{"id":"NCERT12-PHY-RAY-OPTICS-016","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Ray Optics","qno":16,"subject":"Physics","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Ray Optics","type":"mcq","question":"Assertion (A): A glass lens of refractive index 1.52 becomes practically invisible when it is fully dipped in a liquid of refractive index 1.52.\nReason (R): When the refractive index of the lens equals that of the surrounding medium, the lens maker's formula gives $\\frac{1}{f} = 0$, so the lens behaves like a plane sheet of glass.","image":"","options":["Both A and R are true and R is the correct explanation of A","Both A and R are true but R is not the correct explanation of A","A is true but R is false","A is false but R is true"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"A is true: when the two refractive indices are equal, light does not bend at the lens surfaces, so the lens cannot be seen. This is the magician's trick described in NCERT.\nR is true: with $n_2 = n_1$, the factor $(n_2 - n_1)$ is zero, so $\\frac{1}{f} = 0$ and f tends to infinity. The lens acts like a plane glass sheet.\nR directly explains A.\nAnswer: (1)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Physics, Ch 9 · Ray Optics and Optical Instruments","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT12-PHY-RAY-OPTICS-017","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Ray Optics","qno":17,"subject":"Physics","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Ray Optics","type":"mcq","question":"A small mark on a table is viewed from directly above from a height of 40 cm. A glass slab 9 cm thick (refractive index 1.5) is then placed flat on the table over the mark. By how much does the mark appear to be raised?","image":"","options":["3 cm","6 cm","4.5 cm","1.5 cm"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"Given: slab thickness t = 9 cm, n = 1.5\nFormula: the slab's apparent thickness is $\\frac{t}{n}$, so the apparent shift is $t\\left(1 - \\frac{1}{n}\\right)$.\nSubstituting: shift $= 9\\left(1 - \\frac{1}{1.5}\\right) = 9 \\times \\frac{1}{3}$\nSo the mark appears raised by 3 cm. The 40 cm viewing height does not enter the result. The value 6 cm is the apparent thickness of the slab, not the shift.\nAnswer: (1)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Physics, Ch 9 · Ray Optics and Optical Instruments","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT12-PHY-RAY-OPTICS-018","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Ray Optics","qno":18,"subject":"Physics","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Ray Optics","type":"mcq","question":"A ray parallel to the principal axis strikes a concave mirror of radius of curvature 20 cm at a height of 12 cm above the axis. This ray is not paraxial. At what distance from the pole does the reflected ray cross the principal axis?","image":"","options":["7.5 cm","10 cm","12.5 cm","8.0 cm"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"Given: R = 20 cm, h = 12 cm. The radius CM is the normal, so $\\sin\\theta = \\frac{h}{R} = 0.6$ and $\\cos\\theta = 0.8$.\nGeometry: in triangle CMF, angle MCF = θ (alternate angles) and angle CMF = θ (law of reflection). So the triangle is isosceles with CF = MF.\nFormula: $CF = \\frac{R}{2\\cos\\theta} = \\frac{20}{1.6}$ = 12.5 cm.\nSo PF = R − CF = 20 − 12.5 = 7.5 cm. This is less than the paraxial value of 10 cm.\nAnswer: (1)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Physics, Ch 9 · Ray Optics and Optical Instruments","difficulty":"hard"},{"id":"NCERT12-PHY-RAY-OPTICS-019","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Ray Optics","qno":19,"subject":"Physics","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Ray Optics","type":"mcq","question":"Match List-I with List-II.\nList-I\n(A) Magnifying power of a telescope in normal adjustment\n(B) Magnifying power of a simple microscope with the image at infinity\n(C) Magnifying power of a compound microscope with the final image at infinity\n(D) Minimum deviation produced by a thin prism\nList-II\n(i) $\\frac{D}{f}$\n(ii) $\\frac{f_o}{f_e}$\n(iii) $(n-1)A$\n(iv) $\\frac{L}{f_o}\\cdot\\frac{D}{f_e}$","image":"","options":["A-(ii), B-(i), C-(iv), D-(iii)","A-(iv), B-(i), C-(ii), D-(iii)","A-(ii), B-(iii), C-(iv), D-(i)","A-(i), B-(ii), C-(iv), D-(iii)"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"(A) Telescope: $m = \\frac{\\beta}{\\alpha} = \\frac{f_o}{f_e}$, so (ii).\n(B) Simple microscope with the image at infinity: $m = \\frac{D}{f}$, so (i).\n(C) Compound microscope: $m = m_o m_e = \\frac{L}{f_o}\\cdot\\frac{D}{f_e}$, so (iv).\n(D) Thin prism: $D_m = (n-1)A$, so (iii).\nAnswer: (1)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Physics, Ch 9 · Ray Optics and Optical Instruments","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT12-PHY-RAY-OPTICS-020","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Ray Optics","qno":20,"subject":"Physics","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Ray Optics","type":"mcq","question":"Assertion (A): The image of a pencil lying along the principal axis of a concave mirror (beyond C) is distorted.\nReason (R): Different points of the pencil are at different object distances, so they are magnified by different amounts.","image":"","options":["Both A and R are true and R is the correct explanation of A","Both A and R are true but R is not the correct explanation of A","A is true but R is false","A is false but R is true"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"A is true: the image of an object lying along the axis is not a uniformly scaled copy of the object.\nR is true: magnification is $m = -\\frac{v}{u}$, and v depends non-linearly on u.\nEnds of the pencil at different u are therefore imaged with different magnifications. The pencil's length is also not scaled by the same factor as its height.\nThis non-uniform magnification is exactly what causes the distortion, so R explains A.\nAnswer: (1)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Physics, Ch 9 · Ray Optics and Optical Instruments","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT12-PHY-RAY-OPTICS-021","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Ray Optics","qno":21,"subject":"Physics","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Ray Optics","type":"mcq","question":"A laser beam is sent upward through slightly turbid water in a glass beaker so that it strikes the upper water surface. Two spots of light are seen: one on the table and one on the roof. The beam is then tilted until it strikes the surface very obliquely and total internal reflection just occurs. What happens to the two spots?","image":"","options":["Both spots disappear","The spot on the table disappears, and the spot on the roof remains","The spot on the roof disappears, and the spot on the table remains","Both spots remain, but the spot on the roof becomes brighter"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["3"],"explanation":"The spot on the roof is formed by light refracted out into the air. The spot on the table is formed by light reflected back into the water.\nIn total internal reflection, no light is transmitted, so the refracted beam vanishes and the roof spot disappears.\nAll the light is now reflected, so the table spot remains, and it becomes brighter.\nAnswer: (3)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Physics, Ch 9 · Ray Optics and Optical Instruments","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT12-PHY-RAY-OPTICS-022","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Ray Optics","qno":22,"subject":"Physics","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Ray Optics","type":"mcq","question":"A parallel beam of paraxial rays in air falls on a solid glass sphere of radius 10 cm and refractive index 1.5. Treat refraction at each spherical surface separately. At what distance from the far (second) surface of the sphere are the rays finally brought to a focus?","image":"","options":["5 cm","10 cm","15 cm","30 cm"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"First surface: $u = -\\infty$, $R = +10$ cm. $\\frac{1.5}{v_1} = \\frac{0.5}{10}$, so $v_1 = +30$ cm from the first surface.\nThis point is $30 - 20 = 10$ cm beyond the second surface. It acts as a virtual object, $u_2 = +10$ cm.\nSecond surface: glass to air, $n_1 = 1.5$, $n_2 = 1$, $R = -10$ cm.\n$\\frac{1}{v} - \\frac{1.5}{10} = \\frac{-0.5}{-10} = 0.05$, so $\\frac{1}{v} = 0.2$ and $v = +5$ cm.\nThe focus is 5 cm beyond the far surface.\nAnswer: (1)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Physics, Ch 9 · Ray Optics and Optical Instruments","difficulty":"hard"},{"id":"NCERT12-PHY-RAY-OPTICS-023","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Ray Optics","qno":23,"subject":"Physics","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Ray Optics","type":"mcq","question":"An astronomical refracting telescope has an objective of focal length 120 cm and an eyepiece of focal length 4 cm. What is its magnifying power in normal adjustment, when the final image is at infinity?","image":"","options":["30","124","116","480"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"Given: $f_o$ = 120 cm, $f_e$ = 4 cm, final image at infinity.\nFormula: $m = \\frac{f_o}{f_e}$\nSubstituting: $m = \\frac{120}{4}$\nSo m = 30.\n124 is the tube length $f_o + f_e$ in cm, not the magnifying power.\nAnswer: (1)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Physics, Ch 9 · Ray Optics and Optical Instruments","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT12-PHY-RAY-OPTICS-024","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Ray Optics","qno":24,"subject":"Physics","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Ray Optics","type":"mcq","question":"How many of the following statements about spherical mirrors are correct?\n(i) A parallel paraxial beam inclined to the principal axis is brought by a concave mirror to a point lying in its focal plane.\n(ii) For a convex mirror, the reflected rays of a beam parallel to the axis actually meet at F behind the mirror.\n(iii) The normal at any point of a spherical mirror passes through its centre of curvature.\n(iv) For paraxial rays, the principal focus lies midway between the pole and the centre of curvature.\n(v) The focal plane is the plane through the centre of curvature perpendicular to the principal axis.","image":"","options":["1","2","3","4"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["3"],"explanation":"(i) Correct: the inclined beam converges to a point in the focal plane.\n(ii) Incorrect: for a convex mirror the reflected rays only appear to diverge from F. They never actually meet there.\n(iii) Correct: the normal lies along the radius.\n(iv) Correct: since f = R/2, F is midway between P and C.\n(v) Incorrect: the focal plane passes through F, not C.\nThree statements are correct.\nAnswer: (3)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Physics, Ch 9 · Ray Optics and Optical Instruments","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT12-PHY-RAY-OPTICS-025","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Ray Optics","qno":25,"subject":"Physics","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Ray Optics","type":"mcq","question":"An optician combines a convex lens of power $+2.5$ D with a thin concave lens of focal length 25 cm, the two placed in contact. What is the focal length of the combination?","image":"","options":["$-66.7$ cm","$+66.7$ cm","$-15.4$ cm","$+40$ cm"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"Given: $P_1 = +2.5$ D and $f_2 = -0.25$ m, so $P_2 = -4$ D.\nFormula: $P = P_1 + P_2$ and $f = \\frac{1}{P}$.\nSubstituting: $P = 2.5 - 4 = -1.5$ D, so $f = \\frac{1}{-1.5}$ m $= -0.667$ m.\nSo f = $-66.7$ cm. The combination is diverging.\nAnswer: (1)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Physics, Ch 9 · Ray Optics and Optical Instruments","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT12-PHY-RAY-OPTICS-026","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Ray Optics","qno":26,"subject":"Physics","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Ray Optics","type":"mcq","question":"Match List-I with List-II for a spherical mirror.\nList-I:\n(A) Pole\n(B) Centre of curvature\n(C) Principal axis\n(D) Focal plane\nList-II:\n(i) Plane through F, normal to the principal axis\n(ii) Geometric centre of the mirror surface\n(iii) Line joining the pole and the centre of curvature\n(iv) Centre of the sphere of which the mirror is a part","image":"","options":["A-(ii), B-(iv), C-(iii), D-(i)","A-(iv), B-(ii), C-(iii), D-(i)","A-(ii), B-(iv), C-(i), D-(iii)","A-(iii), B-(iv), C-(ii), D-(i)"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"A: The pole is the geometric centre of the mirror, so A-(ii).\nB: The centre of curvature is the centre of the parent sphere, so B-(iv).\nC: The principal axis joins the pole and the centre of curvature, so C-(iii).\nD: Inclined parallel paraxial rays focus in the plane through F perpendicular to the axis, so D-(i).\nAnswer: (1)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Physics, Ch 9 · Ray Optics and Optical Instruments","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT12-PHY-RAY-OPTICS-027","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Ray Optics","qno":27,"subject":"Physics","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Ray Optics","type":"mcq","question":"The critical angle for a transparent medium with respect to air is found to be $30^\\circ$. What is the refractive index of the medium?","image":"","options":["1.50","2.00","1.73","1.15"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["2"],"explanation":"Given: $i_c = 30^\\circ$, and the rarer medium is air.\nFormula: $n = \\frac{1}{\\sin i_c}$\nSubstituting: $n = \\frac{1}{\\sin 30^\\circ} = \\frac{1}{0.5}$\nSo n = 2.00.\nAnswer: (2)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Physics, Ch 9 · Ray Optics and Optical Instruments","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT12-PHY-RAY-OPTICS-028","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Ray Optics","qno":28,"subject":"Physics","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Ray Optics","type":"mcq","question":"A thin convex lens made of glass (n = 1.5) has a focal length of 15 cm in air. What is its focal length when it is completely immersed in water of refractive index $\\frac{4}{3}$?","image":"","options":["60 cm","15 cm","20 cm","45 cm"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"Given: n_glass = 1.5, n_water = 4/3, f_air = 15 cm.\nFormula: $\\frac{1}{f} = \\left(\\frac{n_2}{n_1} - 1\\right)\\left(\\frac{1}{R_1} - \\frac{1}{R_2}\\right)$, with the radii unchanged.\nRatio: $\\frac{f_w}{f_a} = \\frac{1.5 - 1}{\\frac{1.5}{4/3} - 1} = \\frac{0.5}{0.125} = 4$\nSo f_w = 4 × 15 = 60 cm. The lens is still converging, but much weaker in water.\nAnswer: (1)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Physics, Ch 9 · Ray Optics and Optical Instruments","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT12-PHY-RAY-OPTICS-029","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Ray Optics","qno":29,"subject":"Physics","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Ray Optics","type":"mcq","question":"An equiconvex lens is made of glass of refractive index 1.6. Each face has a radius of curvature of 24 cm. What is the focal length of the lens in air?","image":"","options":["20 cm","40 cm","12 cm","30 cm"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"Given: $n = 1.6$, $R_1 = +24$ cm, $R_2 = -24$ cm.\nFormula: $\\frac{1}{f} = (n-1)\\left(\\frac{1}{R_1} - \\frac{1}{R_2}\\right)$\nSubstituting: $\\frac{1}{f} = 0.6\\left(\\frac{1}{24} + \\frac{1}{24}\\right) = \\frac{0.6}{12} = 0.05\\ \\text{cm}^{-1}$\nSo $f$ = 20 cm.\nAnswer: (1)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Physics, Ch 9 · Ray Optics and Optical Instruments","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT12-PHY-RAY-OPTICS-030","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Ray Optics","qno":30,"subject":"Physics","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Ray Optics","type":"mcq","question":"A ray of light travelling parallel to the principal axis falls on a thin concave lens. Which statement correctly describes the refracted ray?","image":"","options":["It actually passes through the second principal focus $F'$ on the far side of the lens","It appears to diverge from the first principal focus $F$, which lies on the same side as the incident light","It emerges without any deviation","It emerges parallel to the principal axis on the other side"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["2"],"explanation":"NCERT ray rule: a ray parallel to the principal axis passes through $F'$ after refraction in a convex lens. In a concave lens it appears to diverge from the first principal focus $F$.\nOption (1) describes a convex lens, not a concave lens.\nAn undeviated ray is one that passes through the optical centre.\nA ray that emerges parallel to the axis is one directed towards a focus, not one that is already parallel.\nAnswer: (2)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Physics, Ch 9 · Ray Optics and Optical Instruments","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT12-PHY-RAY-OPTICS-031","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Ray Optics","qno":31,"subject":"Physics","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Ray Optics","type":"mcq","question":"The average distance between the Sun and the Earth is $1.5 \\times 10^{11}$ m. Taking the speed of light in vacuum as $3 \\times 10^{8}$ m/s, how long does sunlight take to reach the Earth?","image":"","options":["50 s","500 s","5000 s","8.3 s"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["2"],"explanation":"Given: d = $1.5 \\times 10^{11}$ m and c = $3 \\times 10^{8}$ m/s.\nFormula: $t = \\frac{d}{c}$.\nSubstituting: $t = \\frac{1.5 \\times 10^{11}}{3 \\times 10^{8}}$ = 500 s.\nSo t = 500 s, which is about 8.3 minutes. The 8.3 in option (4) is in minutes, not seconds.\nAnswer: (2)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Physics, Ch 9 · Ray Optics and Optical Instruments","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT12-PHY-RAY-OPTICS-032","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Ray Optics","qno":32,"subject":"Physics","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Ray Optics","type":"mcq","question":"Assertion (A): In the graph of angle of deviation ($\\delta$) versus angle of incidence ($i$) for a prism, any value of $\\delta$ greater than $D_m$ is obtained for two different angles of incidence.\nReason (R): The relation $\\delta = i + e - A$ is symmetric in $i$ and $e$, because the path of a ray through the prism can be reversed and gives the same deviation.","image":"","options":["Both A and R are true, and R is the correct explanation of A","Both A and R are true, but R is not the correct explanation of A","A is true but R is false","A is false but R is true"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"A is true: the $\\delta$–$i$ curve has a single minimum at $D_m$. Every other value of $\\delta$ occurs at two values of $i$.\nR is true: swapping $i$ and $e$ leaves $\\delta = i + e - A$ unchanged. Physically, this is because the ray path can be traced back through the prism.\nR explains A: the two angles of incidence giving the same $\\delta$ are just $i$ and $e$ interchanged.\nAnswer: (1)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Physics, Ch 9 · Ray Optics and Optical Instruments","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT12-PHY-RAY-OPTICS-033","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Ray Optics","qno":33,"subject":"Physics","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Ray Optics","type":"mcq","question":"Match List-I (arrangement) with List-II (magnifying power). Here D = least distance of distinct vision, f = focal length of the simple microscope lens, $f_o$ and $f_e$ = focal lengths of objective and eyepiece, and L = tube length.\nList-I\n(A) Simple microscope, final image at infinity\n(B) Compound microscope, final image at infinity\n(C) Astronomical telescope in normal adjustment\n(D) Simple microscope, final image at near point\nList-II\n(I) $\\frac{f_o}{f_e}$\n(II) $1+\\frac{D}{f}$\n(III) $\\frac{D}{f}$\n(IV) $\\frac{L}{f_o}\\cdot\\frac{D}{f_e}$","image":"","options":["A-III, B-IV, C-I, D-II","A-II, B-IV, C-I, D-III","A-III, B-I, C-IV, D-II","A-II, B-I, C-IV, D-III"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"(A) Simple microscope with the image at infinity: $m = D/f$, which is (III).\n(B) Compound microscope with the image at infinity: $m = \\frac{L}{f_o}\\cdot\\frac{D}{f_e}$, which is (IV).\n(C) Telescope in normal adjustment: $m = f_o/f_e$, which is (I).\n(D) Simple microscope with the image at the near point: $m = 1 + D/f$, which is (II).\nThe tempting swap of A and D fails because the near-point value is one more than the infinity value.\nAnswer: (1)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Physics, Ch 9 · Ray Optics and Optical Instruments","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT12-PHY-RAY-OPTICS-034","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Ray Optics","qno":34,"subject":"Physics","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Ray Optics","type":"mcq","question":"How many of the following statements are correct?\n(i) Among water, crown glass, dense flint glass and diamond, diamond has the smallest critical angle with respect to air.\n(ii) A prism that bends light by 90° using total internal reflection needs a material whose critical angle is less than 45°.\n(iii) During total internal reflection, a small fraction of the light is still transmitted into the rarer medium.\n(iv) In an optical fibre, the core has a lower refractive index than the cladding.\n(v) The critical angle for a given pair of media depends on the wavelength of light.","image":"","options":["2","3","4","5"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["2"],"explanation":"(i) Correct: diamond has the largest refractive index (2.42), so its critical angle is the smallest (about 24.4°).\n(ii) Correct: light meets the face at 45°, so total reflection requires $i_c < 45°$.\n(iii) Incorrect: in total internal reflection, no light is transmitted.\n(iv) Incorrect: the core has the higher refractive index.\n(v) Correct: $\\sin i_c = n_{21}$, and $n_{21}$ depends on wavelength.\nSo 3 statements are correct.\nAnswer: (2)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Physics, Ch 9 · Ray Optics and Optical Instruments","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT12-PHY-RAY-OPTICS-035","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Ray Optics","qno":35,"subject":"Physics","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Ray Optics","type":"mcq","question":"Statement I: Turpentine has a lower mass density than water, yet it is optically denser than water.\nStatement II: A medium that is optically denser must always have a greater mass density.","image":"","options":["Both Statement I and Statement II are true","Both Statement I and Statement II are false","Statement I is true but Statement II is false","Statement I is false but Statement II is true"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["3"],"explanation":"Statement I: true. NCERT gives turpentine as the example: it is lighter than water (lower mass per unit volume) but has the higher refractive index.\nStatement II: false. Optical density depends on the speed of light in the medium, not on mass per unit volume. Turpentine itself is a counter-example.\nAnswer: (3)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Physics, Ch 9 · Ray Optics and Optical Instruments","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT12-PHY-RAY-OPTICS-036","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Ray Optics","qno":36,"subject":"Physics","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Ray Optics","type":"mcq","question":"A real point object in air faces a concave spherical glass surface (refractive index 1.5). The centre of curvature lies on the air side. How many of the following statements are correct?\n(i) $R$ must be taken as negative in $\\frac{n_2}{v} - \\frac{n_1}{u} = \\frac{n_2 - n_1}{R}$.\n(ii) The image is always virtual, whatever the object distance.\n(iii) The image is formed on the same side as the object (in air).\n(iv) The relation cannot be used here because it was derived for a convex surface.","image":"","options":["1","2","3","4"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["3"],"explanation":"(i) Correct: C lies opposite to the direction of incident light, so $R < 0$.\n(ii) Correct: $\\frac{n_2}{v} = \\frac{n_2 - n_1}{R} + \\frac{n_1}{u}$. Both terms on the right are negative, so $v < 0$ always.\n(iii) Correct: negative $v$ places the image on the incident (air) side.\n(iv) Incorrect: NCERT states the relation holds for any spherical surface when signs are used properly.\nThree statements are correct.\nAnswer: (3)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Physics, Ch 9 · Ray Optics and Optical Instruments","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT12-PHY-RAY-OPTICS-037","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Ray Optics","qno":37,"subject":"Physics","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Ray Optics","type":"mcq","question":"A point source in air is placed 30 cm in front of a convex spherical glass surface. The glass has refractive index 1.5 and the surface has a radius of curvature of 10 cm. Where is the image formed?","image":"","options":["90 cm inside the glass","60 cm inside the glass","45 cm inside the glass","30 cm inside the glass"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"Given: $n_1 = 1$, $n_2 = 1.5$, $u = -30$ cm, $R = +10$ cm (the centre of curvature lies on the glass side).\nFormula: $\\frac{n_2}{v} - \\frac{n_1}{u} = \\frac{n_2 - n_1}{R}$\nSubstituting: $\\frac{1.5}{v} + \\frac{1}{30} = \\frac{0.5}{10} = 0.05$, so $\\frac{1.5}{v} = 0.0167$.\nSo $v = +90$ cm. This is a real image 90 cm inside the glass.\nAnswer: (1)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Physics, Ch 9 · Ray Optics and Optical Instruments","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT12-PHY-RAY-OPTICS-038","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Ray Optics","qno":38,"subject":"Physics","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Ray Optics","type":"mcq","question":"A vessel contains a 12 cm layer of water (refractive index $\\frac{4}{3}$). A 6 cm layer of oil (refractive index 1.5) floats on top of the water. Viewed from directly above (near-normal viewing), how far below the top surface of the oil does the bottom of the vessel appear?","image":"","options":["13 cm","15 cm","13.5 cm","12 cm"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"Given: water layer = 12 cm with n = $\\frac{4}{3}$; oil layer = 6 cm with n = 1.5\nFormula: each layer appears $\\frac{\\text{thickness}}{n}$ thick, and the apparent depths add.\nSubstituting: $\\frac{12}{4/3} + \\frac{6}{1.5} = 9 + 4$\nSo the bottom appears 13 cm below the oil surface. The value 15 cm ignores refraction in the oil, and 13.5 cm wrongly uses $\\frac{4}{3}$ for both layers.\nAnswer: (1)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Physics, Ch 9 · Ray Optics and Optical Instruments","difficulty":"hard"},{"id":"NCERT12-PHY-RAY-OPTICS-039","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Ray Optics","qno":39,"subject":"Physics","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Ray Optics","type":"mcq","question":"How many of the following statements are correct?\n(i) The critical angle is a property of the pair of media, not of one medium alone.\n(ii) For a prism that bends light by $90^\\circ$ using total internal reflection, the critical angle of the prism material must be less than $45^\\circ$.\n(iii) Total internal reflection can occur when light passes from air into water at a large enough angle of incidence.\n(iv) At an angle of incidence equal to the critical angle, the refracted ray grazes along the interface.\n(v) An optically denser medium has a larger critical angle with respect to air.","image":"","options":["2","3","4","5"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["2"],"explanation":"(i) Correct: $\\sin i_c = n_{21}$ depends on both media.\n(ii) Correct: the ray meets the hypotenuse face at $45^\\circ$, so TIR needs $i_c < 45^\\circ$.\n(iii) Incorrect: air to water is rarer to denser, so TIR is impossible.\n(iv) Correct: at $i = i_c$, the angle of refraction is $90^\\circ$.\n(v) Incorrect: a larger n gives a smaller $i_c$, as in diamond ($24.4^\\circ$) versus water ($48.8^\\circ$).\nThree statements are correct.\nAnswer: (2)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Physics, Ch 9 · Ray Optics and Optical Instruments","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT12-PHY-RAY-OPTICS-040","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Ray Optics","qno":40,"subject":"Physics","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Ray Optics","type":"mcq","question":"Match the object position for a concave mirror in List-I with the image formed in List-II.\nList-I\n(A) Object at infinity\n(B) Object at C (centre of curvature)\n(C) Object between F and C\n(D) Object between P and F\nList-II\n(I) Virtual, erect, magnified, behind the mirror\n(II) Real, inverted, highly diminished, at F\n(III) Real, inverted, magnified, beyond C\n(IV) Real, inverted, same size, at C","image":"","options":["A-II, B-IV, C-III, D-I","A-II, B-III, C-IV, D-I","A-IV, B-II, C-III, D-I","A-II, B-IV, C-I, D-III"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"(A) Parallel rays converge at F, giving a point-sized real image: II.\n(B) For $u = 2f$, the mirror equation gives $v = 2f$ and $m = -1$. The image is real, inverted, same size, at C: IV.\n(C) For $f < |u| < 2f$, we get $|v| > 2f$ and $|m| > 1$. The image is real, inverted, magnified, beyond C: III.\n(D) For $|u| < f$, $v$ is positive and $m > 1$. The image is virtual, erect, magnified: I.\nAnswer: (1)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Physics, Ch 9 · Ray Optics and Optical Instruments","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT12-PHY-RAY-OPTICS-041","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Ray Optics","qno":41,"subject":"Physics","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Ray Optics","type":"mcq","question":"In NCERT's derivation of refraction at a convex spherical surface, a ray from object O strikes the surface at N. C is the centre of curvature and I is the image. Consider the following:\nStatement I: The angle of incidence is $i = \\angle NOM + \\angle NCM$, because $i$ is an exterior angle of triangle NOC.\nStatement II: The angle of refraction is $r = \\angle NCM - \\angle NIM$.\nChoose the correct option.","image":"","options":["Both Statement I and Statement II are correct","Statement I is correct but Statement II is incorrect","Statement I is incorrect but Statement II is correct","Both Statement I and Statement II are incorrect"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"Statement I: Correct. In triangle NOC, the angle of incidence $i$ is the exterior angle at N. So it equals the sum of the two opposite interior angles, $\\angle NOM + \\angle NCM$.\nStatement II: Correct. In triangle NCI, $\\angle NCM$ is the exterior angle, so $\\angle NCM = r + \\angle NIM$. This gives $r = \\angle NCM - \\angle NIM$.\nSubstituting the small-angle forms of these angles into $n_1 i = n_2 r$ gives Eq. (9.15).\nAnswer: (1)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Physics, Ch 9 · Ray Optics and Optical Instruments","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT12-PHY-RAY-OPTICS-042","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Ray Optics","qno":42,"subject":"Physics","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Ray Optics","type":"mcq","question":"Light from a point source in air falls on a convex spherical glass surface ($n = 1.5$, radius of curvature $20$ cm).\nStatement I: When the source is $100$ cm from the surface, a real image is formed $100$ cm inside the glass.\nStatement II: When the source is moved to $40$ cm from the surface, the image becomes virtual and lies on the same side as the source.","image":"","options":["Both Statement I and Statement II are true","Both Statement I and Statement II are false","Statement I is true but Statement II is false","Statement I is false but Statement II is true"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["3"],"explanation":"Use $\\frac{n_2}{v} - \\frac{n_1}{u} = \\frac{n_2 - n_1}{R}$ with $n_1 = 1$, $n_2 = 1.5$, $R = +20$ cm.\nStatement I: $u = -100$ cm gives $1.5/v + 0.01 = 0.025$, so $v = +100$ cm. The image is real and inside the glass. True.\nStatement II: $u = -40$ cm gives $1.5/v + 0.025 = 0.025$, so $1.5/v = 0$ and $v = \\infty$. The source is at the first focus and the refracted rays travel parallel. The image is at infinity, not virtual. False.\nAnswer: (3)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Physics, Ch 9 · Ray Optics and Optical Instruments","difficulty":"hard"},{"id":"NCERT12-PHY-RAY-OPTICS-043","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Ray Optics","qno":43,"subject":"Physics","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Ray Optics","type":"mcq","question":"A point source in air is 20 cm in front of the pole of a concave spherical surface of a large glass block ($n = 1.5$). The surface is concave as seen from the air, and its radius of curvature is 10 cm, so its centre of curvature lies in the air. Light travels from the air into the glass. Where is the image formed, and what is its nature?","image":"","options":["$v = -15$ cm; virtual, on the same side as the object","$v = +15$ cm; real, inside the glass","$v = -10$ cm; virtual, on the same side as the object","$v = -30$ cm; virtual, on the same side as the object"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"Given: $n_1 = 1$, $n_2 = 1.5$, $u = -20$ cm. The centre of curvature lies against the incident light, so $R = -10$ cm.\nFormula: $\\frac{n_2}{v}-\\frac{n_1}{u}=\\frac{n_2-n_1}{R}$\nSubstituting: $\\frac{1.5}{v}+\\frac{1}{20}=\\frac{0.5}{-10}=-0.05$, so $\\frac{1.5}{v}=-0.10$.\nSo $v = -15$ cm. The negative sign means the image lies in the air, on the object's side. The refracted rays only appear to diverge from this point, so the image is virtual.\nOption (3) results from forgetting $n_2$ in $\\frac{n_2}{v}$.\nAnswer: (1)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Physics, Ch 9 · Ray Optics and Optical Instruments","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT12-PHY-RAY-OPTICS-044","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Ray Optics","qno":44,"subject":"Physics","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Ray Optics","type":"mcq","question":"How many of the following statements are correct according to the Cartesian sign convention for spherical mirrors?\n(i) For a real object placed in front of the mirror, u is negative.\n(ii) The focal length of a concave mirror is negative.\n(iii) A real image formed by a concave mirror has a positive magnification.\n(iv) A virtual image formed by a mirror has a positive image distance.\n(v) The height of an inverted image is taken as negative.","image":"","options":["2","3","4","5"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["3"],"explanation":"(i) Correct: the object lies opposite to the direction of incident light.\n(ii) Correct: the focus of a concave mirror lies in front of the mirror.\n(iii) Incorrect: a real image is inverted, so h′ is negative and m is negative.\n(iv) Correct: a virtual image lies behind the mirror, so v is positive.\n(v) Correct: heights measured downwards from the axis are negative.\nFour statements are correct.\nAnswer: (3)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Physics, Ch 9 · Ray Optics and Optical Instruments","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT12-PHY-RAY-OPTICS-045","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Ray Optics","qno":45,"subject":"Physics","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Ray Optics","type":"mcq","question":"An object and a screen are kept 100 cm apart. A convex lens placed between them forms a sharp image on the screen at two positions of the lens that are 60 cm apart. What is the ratio of the size of the larger image to the size of the smaller image?","image":"","options":["16","4","2.67","9"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"Given: u + v = 100 cm (magnitudes) and lens shift d = 60 cm.\nBy the reversibility of light, the two positions simply interchange u and v. So v – u = 60 cm, which gives v = 80 cm and u = 20 cm.\nMagnifications: in the first position |m1| = 80/20 = 4; in the second, |m2| = 20/80 = 1/4.\nSo the ratio of image sizes = 4 ÷ (1/4) = 16.\nCheck: f = uv/(u+v) = 16 cm, which satisfies D > 4f.\nAnswer: (1)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Physics, Ch 9 · Ray Optics and Optical Instruments","difficulty":"hard"},{"id":"NCERT12-PHY-RAY-OPTICS-046","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Ray Optics","qno":46,"subject":"Physics","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Ray Optics","type":"mcq","question":"A prism of refracting angle $60^\\circ$ is made of glass of refractive index $\\sqrt{2}$ and is kept in air. What is the smallest angle of incidence on the first face for which light can still emerge from the second face, instead of suffering total internal reflection there?","image":"","options":["$15.0^\\circ$","$21.5^\\circ$","$30.0^\\circ$","$45.0^\\circ$"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["2"],"explanation":"Given: $A = 60^\\circ$, $n = \\sqrt{2}$, so the critical angle is $i_c = \\sin^{-1}(1/\\sqrt{2}) = 45^\\circ$.\nFormula: for grazing emergence, $r_2 = i_c = 45^\\circ$. Then $r_1 = A - r_2 = 15^\\circ$.\nSubstituting: $\\sin i = n \\sin r_1 = \\sqrt{2} \\times \\sin 15^\\circ = 0.366$.\nSo $i \\approx 21.5^\\circ$.\nIf $i$ is smaller, $r_1$ falls below $15^\\circ$ and $r_2$ exceeds $45^\\circ$, so total internal reflection occurs at the second face.\nAnswer: (2)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Physics, Ch 9 · Ray Optics and Optical Instruments","difficulty":"hard"},{"id":"NCERT12-PHY-RAY-OPTICS-047","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Ray Optics","qno":47,"subject":"Physics","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Ray Optics","type":"mcq","question":"A prism of refracting angle $60^\\circ$ is made of glass of refractive index $\\sqrt{2}$ and is placed in air. What is the minimum angle of incidence on the first face for which the ray just emerges from the second face (grazing emergence) instead of being totally internally reflected?","image":"","options":["$21.5^\\circ$","$45.0^\\circ$","$30.0^\\circ$","$15.0^\\circ$"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"Critical angle: $\\sin i_c = \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}}$, so $i_c = 45^\\circ$. For grazing emergence, $r_2 = 45^\\circ$.\nFrom $r_1 + r_2 = A$: $r_1 = 60^\\circ - 45^\\circ = 15^\\circ$.\nSnell's law at the first face: $\\sin i = \\sqrt{2}\\sin 15^\\circ = 1.414 \\times 0.259 = 0.366$.\nSo $i \\approx 21.5^\\circ$.\nFor smaller $i$, $r_2$ exceeds $45^\\circ$ and TIR occurs. Option (4), $15^\\circ$, is $r_1$, not $i$.\nAnswer: (1)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Physics, Ch 9 · Ray Optics and Optical Instruments","difficulty":"hard"},{"id":"NCERT12-PHY-RAY-OPTICS-048","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Ray Optics","qno":48,"subject":"Physics","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Ray Optics","type":"mcq","question":"A person sitting in a parked car watches a cyclist approaching from behind in a convex side-view mirror of radius of curvature 4 m. The cyclist moves at a constant 10 m/s. What is the average speed of the cyclist's image while the cyclist moves from 22 m to 12 m from the mirror?","image":"","options":["$0.119$ m/s","$0.833$ m/s","$0.060$ m/s","$10$ m/s"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"Given: $f = +2$ m. The cyclist goes from $u_1 = -22$ m to $u_2 = -12$ m, which takes 1 s.\nFormula: $v = \\frac{fu}{u - f}$\nSubstituting: $v_1 = \\frac{2 \\times (-22)}{-24} = \\frac{11}{6}$ m and $v_2 = \\frac{2 \\times (-12)}{-14} = \\frac{12}{7}$ m.\nThe image shift is $\\frac{11}{6} - \\frac{12}{7} = \\frac{5}{42}$ m, covered in 1 s.\nSo the average image speed is about $0.119$ m/s. It is much less than 10 m/s, and it grows as the cyclist comes closer.\nAnswer: (1)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Physics, Ch 9 · Ray Optics and Optical Instruments","difficulty":"hard"},{"id":"NCERT12-PHY-RAY-OPTICS-049","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Ray Optics","qno":49,"subject":"Physics","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Ray Optics","type":"mcq","question":"Match List-I with List-II.\nList-I\n(A) Prism turning light through $90^\\circ$\n(B) Prism turning light through $180^\\circ$\n(C) Optical fibre\n(D) Mirage\nList-II\n(i) A single total internal reflection at the hypotenuse face\n(ii) Two total internal reflections, one at each shorter face\n(iii) Repeated total internal reflections at the core–cladding boundary\n(iv) Total internal reflection by optically rarer hot air layers near the ground\nChoose the correct option.","image":"","options":["A-(i), B-(ii), C-(iii), D-(iv)","A-(ii), B-(i), C-(iii), D-(iv)","A-(i), B-(ii), C-(iv), D-(iii)","A-(ii), B-(i), C-(iv), D-(iii)"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"(A) The light enters normally through a shorter face and is totally reflected once at the hypotenuse. This turns it by $90^\\circ$: (i).\n(B) The light enters normally through the hypotenuse and is totally reflected at both shorter faces. It returns reversed, turned by $180^\\circ$: (ii).\n(C) A fibre guides light by repeated TIR at the core–cladding boundary: (iii).\n(D) In a mirage, light from the sky bends through layers of hot, optically rarer air near the ground and is finally totally reflected: (iv).\nAnswer: (1)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Physics, Ch 9 · Ray Optics and Optical Instruments","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT12-PHY-RAY-OPTICS-050","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Ray Optics","qno":50,"subject":"Physics","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Ray Optics","type":"mcq","question":"Match List-I (arrangement) with List-II (magnifying power):\nList-I\n(A) Simple microscope, image at near point\n(B) Simple microscope, image at infinity\n(C) Compound microscope, final image at infinity\n(D) Astronomical refracting telescope, normal adjustment\nList-II\n(I) $\\frac{f_o}{f_e}$\n(II) $\\frac{D}{f}$\n(III) $1 + \\frac{D}{f}$\n(IV) $\\frac{L}{f_o}\\cdot\\frac{D}{f_e}$","image":"","options":["A-III, B-II, C-IV, D-I","A-II, B-III, C-IV, D-I","A-III, B-II, C-I, D-IV","A-II, B-III, C-I, D-IV"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"(A) Image at near point: $m = 1 + D/f$, which matches III.\n(B) Image at infinity: $m = D/f$, which matches II.\n(C) Compound microscope with final image at infinity: $m = (L/f_o)(D/f_e)$, which matches IV.\n(D) Telescope in normal adjustment: $m = f_o/f_e$, which matches I.\nAnswer: (1)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Physics, Ch 9 · Ray Optics and Optical Instruments","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT12-PHY-RAY-OPTICS-051","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Ray Optics","qno":51,"subject":"Physics","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Ray Optics","type":"mcq","question":"A convex lens $L_1$ (focal length 10 cm) and a convex lens $L_2$ (focal length 20 cm) are placed coaxially 20 cm apart. An object is placed 15 cm to the left of $L_1$, and light travels from left to right. What is the total magnification of the final image produced by the system?","image":"","options":["−1.33","−2.67","+1.33","−0.67"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"Lens $L_1$: $\\frac{1}{v_1} = \\frac{1}{10} + \\frac{1}{-15} = \\frac{1}{30}$, so $v_1$ = +30 cm. Then $m_1 = \\frac{30}{-15}$ = −2.\nThis image lies 30 − 20 = 10 cm to the right of $L_2$. It acts as a virtual object for $L_2$, so $u_2$ = +10 cm.\nLens $L_2$: $\\frac{1}{v_2} = \\frac{1}{20} + \\frac{1}{10} = \\frac{3}{20}$, so $v_2$ = 6.67 cm. Then $m_2 = \\frac{6.67}{10}$ = 0.667.\nTotal: $m = m_1 m_2$ = (−2)(0.667) = −1.33.\nAnswer: (1)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Physics, Ch 9 · Ray Optics and Optical Instruments","difficulty":"hard"},{"id":"NCERT12-PHY-RAY-OPTICS-052","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Ray Optics","qno":52,"subject":"Physics","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Ray Optics","type":"mcq","question":"Match List-I with List-II.\nList-I\n(A) Prism that bends a ray through $90^\\circ$\n(B) Optical fibre\n(C) Cassegrain telescope\n(D) Thin prism\nList-II\n(I) Deviation $\\approx (n-1)A$\n(II) Critical angle of the material must be less than $45^\\circ$\n(III) Core has higher refractive index than cladding\n(IV) Convex secondary mirror sends light through a hole in the primary mirror","image":"","options":["A-II, B-III, C-IV, D-I","A-III, B-II, C-IV, D-I","A-II, B-III, C-I, D-IV","A-IV, B-III, C-II, D-I"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"A-II: Light meets the inner face at $45^\\circ$. Total internal reflection needs $i_c < 45^\\circ$.\nB-III: The core must be optically denser than the cladding so that light is totally internally reflected at the boundary.\nC-IV: A convex secondary mirror reflects the light back through a hole in the concave primary mirror.\nD-I: For a small prism angle, $D_m = (n-1)A$.\nAnswer: (1)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Physics, Ch 9 · Ray Optics and Optical Instruments","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT12-PHY-RAY-OPTICS-053","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Ray Optics","qno":53,"subject":"Physics","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Ray Optics","type":"mcq","question":"Which of the following correctly describes the largest telescope in India, located at Kavalur, Tamil Nadu?","image":"","options":["A 40 inch refracting telescope with a lens objective","A 2.34 m diameter reflecting telescope of Cassegrain type","A pair of 10 m diameter reflecting telescopes","A 200 inch reflector in which the observer sits in a cage at the focus"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["2"],"explanation":"NCERT fact: India's largest telescope is at Kavalur. It is a 2.34 m diameter reflecting telescope of the Cassegrain type, run by the Indian Institute of Astrophysics, Bangalore.\nThe other options describe other telescopes. The 40 inch lens is the Yerkes refractor. The 10 m pair is the Keck telescopes in Hawaii. The 200 inch reflector with an observer cage is at Mt. Palomar.\nAnswer: (2)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Physics, Ch 9 · Ray Optics and Optical Instruments","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT12-PHY-RAY-OPTICS-054","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Ray Optics","qno":54,"subject":"Physics","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Ray Optics","type":"mcq","question":"Assertion (A): Most modern large astronomical telescopes use a concave mirror instead of a lens as the objective.\nReason (R): A mirror has no chromatic aberration, and it can be supported over its entire back surface rather than only at its rim.","image":"","options":["Both A and R are true and R is the correct explanation of A","Both A and R are true but R is not the correct explanation of A","A is true but R is false","A is false but R is true"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"A is true. Modern large telescopes are reflecting telescopes with concave mirror objectives.\nR is true. Reflection does not depend on wavelength, so a mirror has no chromatic aberration. A mirror is also lighter than a lens of equal optical quality and can be supported over its whole back.\nThese are exactly the reasons NCERT gives for preferring mirrors, so R explains A.\nAnswer: (1)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Physics, Ch 9 · Ray Optics and Optical Instruments","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT12-PHY-RAY-OPTICS-055","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Ray Optics","qno":55,"subject":"Physics","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Ray Optics","type":"mcq","question":"The lower half of the reflecting surface of a concave mirror is covered with an opaque, non-reflecting material. An object is placed in front of the mirror at a position where it would normally form a real image. What happens to the image?","image":"","options":["Only the upper half of the object's image is formed, with normal brightness","The complete image of the object is formed, but with reduced intensity","Only the lower half of the object's image is formed, with reduced intensity","No image is formed, because the paraxial rays near the pole are blocked"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["2"],"explanation":"Every point of the object sends rays to all parts of the mirror.\nThe uncovered half still obeys the laws of reflection, so rays from every object point still meet at the corresponding image points. The whole image therefore forms at the same position.\nLess reflecting area means fewer rays reach each image point, so the image is dimmer. Here the intensity is about half.\nOption (1) is the tempting wrong answer: covering part of the mirror does not cut off part of the image.\nAnswer: (2)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Physics, Ch 9 · Ray Optics and Optical Instruments","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT12-PHY-RAY-OPTICS-056","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Ray Optics","qno":56,"subject":"Physics","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Ray Optics","type":"mcq","question":"How many of the following statements about thin lenses are correct?\n(i) The net power of thin lenses in contact is the algebraic sum of their powers.\n(ii) The equivalent focal length of two thin lenses in contact is $f_1 + f_2$.\n(iii) The total magnification of a lens combination is the sum of the individual magnifications.\n(iv) A combination of lenses can enhance the sharpness of the image.\n(v) A lens of focal length −25 cm has a power of −4 D.","image":"","options":["2","3","4","5"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["2"],"explanation":"(i) Correct: $P = P_1 + P_2 + \\ldots$, taken with signs.\n(ii) Incorrect: the reciprocals add, $\\frac{1}{f} = \\frac{1}{f_1} + \\frac{1}{f_2}$, not the focal lengths themselves.\n(iii) Incorrect: the total magnification is the product, $m = m_1 m_2 m_3 \\ldots$\n(iv) Correct: NCERT states that combining lenses enhances image sharpness.\n(v) Correct: P = 1/(−0.25 m) = −4 D.\nThree statements are correct.\nAnswer: (2)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Physics, Ch 9 · Ray Optics and Optical Instruments","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT12-PHY-RAY-OPTICS-057","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Ray Optics","qno":57,"subject":"Physics","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Ray Optics","type":"mcq","question":"A student uses a convex lens of focal length 4 cm as a simple magnifier. The lens is adjusted so that the virtual image forms at the near point, $D = 25$ cm. What is the magnification produced?","image":"","options":["7.25","6.25","5.25","4.00"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"Given: $f = 4$ cm, image at the near point, $D = 25$ cm.\nFormula: $m = 1 + \\frac{D}{f}$\nSubstituting: $m = 1 + \\frac{25}{4} = 1 + 6.25$\nSo m = 7.25. The value 6.25 is $D/f$, which applies only when the image is at infinity.\nAnswer: (1)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Physics, Ch 9 · Ray Optics and Optical Instruments","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT12-PHY-RAY-OPTICS-058","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Ray Optics","qno":58,"subject":"Physics","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Ray Optics","type":"mcq","question":"A terrestrial telescope differs from an astronomical refracting telescope because it has an additional pair of lenses. What is the purpose of these lenses?","image":"","options":["To increase the light-gathering power","To remove chromatic aberration completely","To make the final image erect","To increase the resolving power"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["3"],"explanation":"An astronomical refracting telescope gives a final image that is inverted. This does not matter for stars, but it is inconvenient when viewing objects on Earth.\nNCERT states that terrestrial telescopes carry an extra pair of inverting lenses so that the final image is erect.\nLight-gathering power and resolving power depend on the diameter of the objective, not on these extra lenses.\nAnswer: (3)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Physics, Ch 9 · Ray Optics and Optical Instruments","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT12-PHY-RAY-OPTICS-059","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Ray Optics","qno":59,"subject":"Physics","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Ray Optics","type":"mcq","question":"How many of the following are based on total internal reflection?\n(i) Transmission of signals through optical fibres\n(ii) A glass prism used to bend a ray by $90^\\circ$\n(iii) Mirage\n(iv) Brilliance (sparkle) of diamond\n(v) Lateral shift of a ray passing through a parallel-sided glass slab\n(vi) The bottom of a water tank appearing raised","image":"","options":["3","4","5","6"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["2"],"explanation":"(i) TIR: light reflects repeatedly at the core-cladding boundary.\n(ii) TIR: the critical angle of glass is below $45^\\circ$, so the ray is totally reflected inside the prism.\n(iii) TIR, as listed in the NCERT summary.\n(iv) TIR: diamond has a small critical angle ($\\approx 24.4^\\circ$), so light undergoes multiple internal reflections.\n(v) Not TIR: the shift is due to simple refraction at two parallel faces.\n(vi) Not TIR: apparent depth is a refraction effect.\nSo 4 items are based on TIR.\nAnswer: (2)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Physics, Ch 9 · Ray Optics and Optical Instruments","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT12-PHY-RAY-OPTICS-060","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Ray Optics","qno":60,"subject":"Physics","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Ray Optics","type":"mcq","question":"A telescope has an objective of focal length 100 cm and an eyepiece of focal length 5 cm. It is adjusted so that the final image of a distant object forms at the least distance of distinct vision, 25 cm. What is its magnifying power?","image":"","options":["20","24","26","16"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["2"],"explanation":"Given: $f_o$ = 100 cm, $f_e$ = 5 cm, D = 25 cm.\nFormula: when the final image is at the near point, $m = \\frac{f_o}{f_e}\\left(1 + \\frac{f_e}{D}\\right)$\nSubstituting: $m = 20 \\times \\left(1 + \\frac{5}{25}\\right) = 20 \\times 1.2$\nSo m = 24.\nThe value 20 is the magnifying power in normal adjustment, which is always smaller than this.\nAnswer: (2)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Physics, Ch 9 · Ray Optics and Optical Instruments","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT12-PHY-RAY-OPTICS-061","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Ray Optics","qno":61,"subject":"Physics","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Ray Optics","type":"mcq","question":"Consider a simple magnifier held very close to the eye.\nStatement I: The angle subtended at the eye by the virtual image equals the angle subtended at the eye by the object at its actual position.\nStatement II: The magnifier gives angular magnification because it makes the image subtend a larger angle at the eye than the object subtends at its own actual position.","image":"","options":["Both Statement I and Statement II are correct","Statement I is correct but Statement II is incorrect","Statement I is incorrect but Statement II is correct","Both Statement I and Statement II are incorrect"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["2"],"explanation":"Statement I is correct. With the eye at the lens, the image and the object subtend the same angle at the eye.\nStatement II is incorrect. The gain is measured against the angle the object would subtend at D = 25 cm, the closest point for clear unaided viewing.\nThe lens lets the object be brought much closer than D, so it subtends a larger angle. The image is still placed where the eye can see it.\nAnswer: (2)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Physics, Ch 9 · Ray Optics and Optical Instruments","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT12-PHY-RAY-OPTICS-062","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Ray Optics","qno":62,"subject":"Physics","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Ray Optics","type":"mcq","question":"A thin convex lens has a focal length of 25 cm. What is its power?","image":"","options":["+4 D","+0.04 D","+25 D","−4 D"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"Given: f = +25 cm = +0.25 m (converging lens, so f is positive).\nFormula: $P = \\frac{1}{f}$, with f in metres.\nSubstituting: $P = \\frac{1}{0.25}$ = 4 $m^{-1}$.\nSo P = +4 D. The value 0.04 comes from using f in cm instead of metres.\nAnswer: (1)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Physics, Ch 9 · Ray Optics and Optical Instruments","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT12-PHY-RAY-OPTICS-063","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Ray Optics","qno":63,"subject":"Physics","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Ray Optics","type":"mcq","question":"A compound microscope must have a magnifying power of 50 with the final image at infinity. The available objective has focal length 1.0 cm and the eyepiece has focal length 2.5 cm. Take $D = 25$ cm. What should be the separation between the objective and the eyepiece?","image":"","options":["8.5 cm","6.0 cm","7.2 cm","11.0 cm"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"Given: m = 50, final image at infinity, $f_o = 1.0$ cm, $f_e = 2.5$ cm.\nEyepiece: $m_e = D/f_e = 10$, so the objective must give $m_o = 5$, i.e. $v_o = -5u_o$.\nObjective: $\\frac{1}{-5u_o} - \\frac{1}{u_o} = \\frac{1}{1.0}$ gives $u_o = -1.2$ cm and $v_o = 6.0$ cm.\nFor a final image at infinity, the first image must lie at the focus of the eyepiece.\nSo separation = $v_o + f_e$ = 6.0 + 2.5 = 8.5 cm.\nAnswer: (1)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Physics, Ch 9 · Ray Optics and Optical Instruments","difficulty":"hard"},{"id":"NCERT12-PHY-RAY-OPTICS-064","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Ray Optics","qno":64,"subject":"Physics","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Ray Optics","type":"mcq","question":"A concave mirror has a radius of curvature of 30 cm. Using the Cartesian sign convention, with light incident from the left, its focal length is:","image":"","options":["$+15$ cm","$-15$ cm","$-30$ cm","$-60$ cm"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["2"],"explanation":"Given: R = 30 cm for a concave mirror.\nFormula: $f = \\frac{R}{2}$, so the magnitude of f is 15 cm.\nSign: the focus of a concave mirror lies in front of the mirror, opposite to the direction of incident light, so f is negative.\nSo f = $-15$ cm.\nAnswer: (2)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Physics, Ch 9 · Ray Optics and Optical Instruments","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT12-PHY-RAY-OPTICS-065","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Ray Optics","qno":65,"subject":"Physics","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Ray Optics","type":"mcq","question":"An optical fibre has a core of refractive index 1.60 and a cladding of refractive index 1.50. What is the minimum angle of incidence at the core–cladding boundary for which light is totally internally reflected?","image":"","options":["$69.6^\\circ$","$20.4^\\circ$","$41.8^\\circ$","$38.7^\\circ$"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"Given: $n_{core}$ = 1.60, $n_{clad}$ = 1.50.\nFormula: $\\sin i_c = \\frac{n_{clad}}{n_{core}}$, since light goes from the core into the cladding.\nSubstituting: $\\sin i_c = \\frac{1.50}{1.60} = 0.9375$, so $i_c \\approx 69.6^\\circ$.\nThe value 38.7° would be the core–air critical angle. Here the second medium is the cladding, not air.\nSo the minimum angle of incidence is $69.6^\\circ$.\nAnswer: (1)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Physics, Ch 9 · Ray Optics and Optical Instruments","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT12-PHY-RAY-OPTICS-066","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Ray Optics","qno":66,"subject":"Physics","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Ray Optics","type":"mcq","question":"An object stands upright in front of a concave mirror, and light falls on the mirror from the left. According to the Cartesian sign convention, which statement is correct?","image":"","options":["The object distance u is positive because the object is in front of the mirror","Distances are measured from the centre of curvature of the mirror","The height of a real inverted image (below the principal axis) is taken as negative","The focal length of the concave mirror is taken as positive"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["3"],"explanation":"(1) Incorrect: the object lies opposite to the direction of incident light, so u is negative.\n(2) Incorrect: all distances are measured from the pole.\n(3) Correct: heights measured downward from the principal axis are negative.\n(4) Incorrect: the focus of a concave mirror lies in front of it, so f is negative.\nAnswer: (3)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Physics, Ch 9 · Ray Optics and Optical Instruments","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT12-PHY-RAY-OPTICS-067","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Ray Optics","qno":67,"subject":"Physics","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Ray Optics","type":"mcq","question":"A compound microscope has an objective of focal length 1.5 cm and an eyepiece of focal length 2.5 cm. The tube length (distance between the second focal point of the objective and the first focal point of the eyepiece) is 15 cm. What is its magnifying power when the final image is formed at infinity? (Take D = 25 cm.)","image":"","options":["110","100","150","60"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["2"],"explanation":"Given: $f_o$ = 1.5 cm, $f_e$ = 2.5 cm, L = 15 cm, D = 25 cm, with the image at infinity.\nFormula: $m = \\left(\\frac{L}{f_o}\\right)\\left(\\frac{D}{f_e}\\right)$.\nSubstituting: $m = \\frac{15}{1.5} \\times \\frac{25}{2.5} = 10 \\times 10 = 100$.\nThe value 110 comes from using $1 + \\frac{D}{f_e}$. That expression applies only when the final image is at the near point.\nAnswer: (2)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Physics, Ch 9 · Ray Optics and Optical Instruments","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT12-PHY-RAY-OPTICS-068","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Ray Optics","qno":68,"subject":"Physics","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Ray Optics","type":"mcq","question":"A ray of light passes through an equilateral glass prism kept in air. The angle of minimum deviation is found to be $30^\\circ$. What is the refractive index of the prism material?","image":"","options":["1.41","1.50","1.73","1.33"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"Given: $A = 60^\\circ$ (equilateral prism), $D_m = 30^\\circ$.\nFormula: $n = \\frac{\\sin[(A + D_m)/2]}{\\sin(A/2)}$\nSubstituting: $n = \\frac{\\sin 45^\\circ}{\\sin 30^\\circ} = \\frac{0.707}{0.5}$\nSo $n = \\sqrt{2} \\approx 1.41$.\nAnswer: (1)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Physics, Ch 9 · Ray Optics and Optical Instruments","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT12-PHY-RAY-OPTICS-069","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Ray Optics","qno":69,"subject":"Physics","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Ray Optics","type":"mcq","question":"How many of the following statements are correct?\n(a) A $45^\\circ$–$90^\\circ$–$45^\\circ$ crown glass prism (n = 1.52) in air can reverse a beam through $180^\\circ$ by total internal reflection.\n(b) The same crown glass prism immersed in water (n = 1.33) can still turn a beam through $90^\\circ$ by total internal reflection at its hypotenuse.\n(c) Light continues to travel along an optical fibre even when the fibre is bent.\n(d) In a decorative fibre lamp, light from the lamp at one end appears as a dot at the tip of the free end of each fibre.","image":"","options":["1","2","3","4"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["3"],"explanation":"(a) Correct: in air, crown glass has $i_c$ = 41.1°, which is less than 45°. The rays meet each face at 45°, so TIR occurs.\n(b) Incorrect: in water, $\\sin i_c = 1.33/1.52 = 0.875$, so $i_c \\approx 61^\\circ$. This exceeds 45°, so light leaks out of the hypotenuse.\n(c) Correct: the fibre is designed so that rays still strike the walls above $i_c$ even when it is bent.\n(d) Correct: this is NCERT's description of the decorative fibre lamp.\nThree statements are correct.\nAnswer: (3)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Physics, Ch 9 · Ray Optics and Optical Instruments","difficulty":"hard"},{"id":"NCERT12-PHY-RAY-OPTICS-070","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Ray Optics","qno":70,"subject":"Physics","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Ray Optics","type":"mcq","question":"A light pipe has a core of refractive index 1.50 surrounded by a cladding of refractive index 1.40. Its flat end face is perpendicular to its axis, and light enters this face from air. What is the largest angle of incidence at the end face (measured from the axis) for which the ray will be totally internally reflected at the core–cladding boundary?","image":"","options":["$32.6^\\circ$","$69.0^\\circ$","$21.0^\\circ$","$53.9^\\circ$"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"Given: $n_1$ = 1.50 (core), $n_2$ = 1.40 (cladding).\nStep 1: At the side wall, $\\sin i_c = n_2/n_1 = 0.933$. TIR needs the wall angle to be at least $i_c$. The refraction angle at the end face is $r = 90^\\circ - (\\text{wall angle})$, so $r \\le 90^\\circ - i_c$.\nStep 2: Snell's law at the end face gives $\\sin i_{max} = n_1 \\cos i_c = \\sqrt{n_1^2 - n_2^2} = \\sqrt{0.29} = 0.539$.\nSo $i_{max} \\approx 32.6^\\circ$.\nAnswer: (1)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Physics, Ch 9 · Ray Optics and Optical Instruments","difficulty":"hard"},{"id":"NCERT12-PHY-RAY-OPTICS-071","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Ray Optics","qno":71,"subject":"Physics","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Ray Optics","type":"mcq","question":"Assertion (A): The image of any real object seen in a convex rear-view mirror is always erect and smaller than the object.\nReason (R): For a convex mirror, with $u$ negative and $f$ positive, the mirror equation always gives a positive $v$ smaller than $f$.","image":"","options":["Both A and R are true and R is the correct explanation of A","Both A and R are true but R is not the correct explanation of A","A is true but R is false","A is false but R is true"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"R: The mirror equation gives $\\frac{1}{v} = \\frac{1}{f} - \\frac{1}{u} = \\frac{1}{f} + \\frac{1}{|u|}$. This is positive and larger than $\\frac{1}{f}$, so $0 < v < f$. R is true.\nA: Since $v > 0$, the image is virtual and lies behind the mirror. Also $m = -\\frac{v}{u} = \\frac{v}{|u|}$ is positive (erect) and less than 1 (diminished). A is true.\nThe sign analysis in R directly leads to A, so R explains A.\nAnswer: (1)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Physics, Ch 9 · Ray Optics and Optical Instruments","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT12-PHY-RAY-OPTICS-072","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Ray Optics","qno":72,"subject":"Physics","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Ray Optics","type":"mcq","question":"A concave mirror has a radius of curvature of 20 cm. An object is placed 30 cm in front of it. Using the Cartesian sign convention, what is the image distance $v$?","image":"","options":["$-15$ cm","$+15$ cm","$-7.5$ cm","$-30$ cm"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"Given: R = 20 cm, so $f = -\\frac{R}{2} = -10$ cm for a concave mirror. The object distance is $u = -30$ cm.\nFormula: $\\frac{1}{v} + \\frac{1}{u} = \\frac{1}{f}$\nSubstituting: $\\frac{1}{v} = -\\frac{1}{10} + \\frac{1}{30} = -\\frac{2}{30}$\nSo v = $-15$ cm. The image is real and forms in front of the mirror.\nAnswer: (1)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Physics, Ch 9 · Ray Optics and Optical Instruments","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT12-PHY-RAY-OPTICS-073","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Ray Optics","qno":73,"subject":"Physics","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Ray Optics","type":"mcq","question":"An object is placed 20 cm in front of a convex mirror of focal length 20 cm. What is the linear magnification produced?","image":"","options":["$+0.5$","$-0.5$","$+2$","$+1$"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"Given: f = +20 cm for a convex mirror, and u = −20 cm.\nFormula: $\\frac{1}{v} = \\frac{1}{f} - \\frac{1}{u}$ and $m = -\\frac{v}{u}$\nSubstituting: $\\frac{1}{v} = \\frac{1}{20} + \\frac{1}{20} = \\frac{1}{10}$, so v = +10 cm.\nThen $m = -\\frac{10}{-20} = +0.5$\nSo m = +0.5. The image is erect, virtual and half the object's size. The tempting value −0.5 comes from forgetting the minus sign in the formula for m.\nAnswer: (1)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Physics, Ch 9 · Ray Optics and Optical Instruments","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT12-PHY-RAY-OPTICS-074","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Ray Optics","qno":74,"subject":"Physics","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Ray Optics","type":"mcq","question":"A concave mirror of focal length 12 cm is used as a shaving mirror. It must produce an erect image three times the size of the face. How far from the mirror should the face be held?","image":"","options":["8 cm","16 cm","4 cm","36 cm"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"Given: f = −12 cm. The image is erect, so it is virtual and m = +3.\nFrom $m = -\\frac{v}{u} = 3$, we get v = −3u.\nMirror equation: $-\\frac{1}{3u} + \\frac{1}{u} = \\frac{2}{3u} = -\\frac{1}{12}$, so u = −8 cm.\nThe face must be 8 cm away, which is between P and F, as expected for a virtual image. Taking m = −3 instead gives 16 cm, but that image is real and inverted.\nAnswer: (1)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Physics, Ch 9 · Ray Optics and Optical Instruments","difficulty":"hard"},{"id":"NCERT12-PHY-RAY-OPTICS-075","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Ray Optics","qno":75,"subject":"Physics","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Ray Optics","type":"mcq","question":"Which of the following statements about thin lenses is INCORRECT?","image":"","options":["The net power of thin lenses in contact is the algebraic sum of their individual powers","The total magnification of a combination of lenses is the product of the individual magnifications","A glass lens of refractive index 1.47 placed in a liquid of refractive index 1.47 behaves like a plane glass sheet","The focal length of a convex glass lens decreases when it is immersed in water"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["4"],"explanation":"(1) Correct: $P = P_1 + P_2 + \\dots$, with signs.\n(2) Correct: the image from one lens is the object for the next, so $m = m_1 m_2 m_3\\dots$\n(3) Correct: with $n_1 = n_2$, the lens maker's formula gives $1/f = 0$. The lens then acts as a plane sheet.\n(4) Incorrect: in water, $(n_2-n_1)/n_1$ becomes smaller, so $f$ increases. For example, 20 cm in air becomes about 78 cm in water.\nAnswer: (4)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Physics, Ch 9 · Ray Optics and Optical Instruments","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT12-PHY-RAY-OPTICS-076","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Ray Optics","qno":76,"subject":"Physics","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Ray Optics","type":"mcq","question":"While drawing a ray diagram for a concave mirror, which incident ray retraces its own path after reflection?","image":"","options":["A ray parallel to the principal axis","A ray passing through the principal focus","A ray passing through the centre of curvature","A ray incident obliquely at the pole"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["3"],"explanation":"A ray through the centre of curvature C travels along a radius. A radius is normal to the mirror surface.\nIts angle of incidence is therefore zero, so it reflects straight back along the same path.\nA parallel ray reflects through F, and a ray through F reflects parallel to the axis.\nA ray striking the pole obliquely reflects symmetrically about the axis. It does not come back along its own path.\nAnswer: (3)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Physics, Ch 9 · Ray Optics and Optical Instruments","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT12-PHY-RAY-OPTICS-077","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Ray Optics","qno":77,"subject":"Physics","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Ray Optics","type":"mcq","question":"An astronomical refracting telescope is used to view a distant star in normal adjustment, so the final image is at infinity. The distance between its objective and eyepiece is 105 cm, and its magnifying power is 20. What is the focal length of the objective lens?","image":"","options":["100 cm","110.5 cm","95 cm","5 cm"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"Given: normal adjustment, tube length L = 105 cm, magnifying power m = 20.\nFormula: in normal adjustment, $L = f_o + f_e$ and $m = \\frac{f_o}{f_e}$, so $f_o = 20f_e$.\nSubstituting: $20f_e + f_e = 105$, which gives $21f_e = 105$ and $f_e = 5$ cm.\nSo $f_o = 20 \\times 5 = 100$ cm. Using $L = f_o - f_e$ by mistake gives 110.5 cm, and 5 cm is the eyepiece focal length, not the objective's.\nAnswer: (1)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Physics, Ch 9 · Ray Optics and Optical Instruments","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT12-PHY-RAY-OPTICS-078","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Ray Optics","qno":78,"subject":"Physics","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Ray Optics","type":"mcq","question":"An object is placed 30 cm to the left of a convex lens $L_1$ of focal length 20 cm. A second convex lens $L_2$, also of focal length 20 cm, is placed coaxially 40 cm to the right of $L_1$. What is the total linear magnification produced by the two-lens system?","image":"","options":["$-1$","$-2$","$+0.5$","$-4$"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"Lens $L_1$: $\\frac{1}{v_1} = \\frac{1}{20} + \\frac{1}{-30} = \\frac{1}{60}$, so $v_1 = +60$ cm. Then $m_1 = \\frac{v_1}{u_1} = \\frac{60}{-30} = -2$.\nThe first image lies 60 - 40 = 20 cm to the right of $L_2$. It acts as a virtual object, so $u_2 = +20$ cm.\nLens $L_2$: $\\frac{1}{v_2} = \\frac{1}{20} + \\frac{1}{20}$, so $v_2 = +10$ cm. Then $m_2 = \\frac{10}{20} = 0.5$.\nTotal magnification: $m = m_1 m_2 = (-2)(0.5) = -1$. The final image is inverted and of the same size.\nAnswer: (1)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Physics, Ch 9 · Ray Optics and Optical Instruments","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT12-PHY-RAY-OPTICS-079","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Ray Optics","qno":79,"subject":"Physics","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Ray Optics","type":"mcq","question":"Match List-I (object position) with List-II (nature of the image) for thin lenses.\nList-I\n(A) Object at $2F$ of a convex lens\n(B) Object between $F$ and the optical centre of a convex lens\n(C) Real object anywhere in front of a concave lens\n(D) Object at $F$ of a convex lens\nList-II\n(i) Image at infinity\n(ii) Virtual, erect, diminished\n(iii) Real, inverted, same size\n(iv) Virtual, erect, magnified","image":"","options":["A-(iii), B-(iv), C-(ii), D-(i)","A-(iii), B-(ii), C-(iv), D-(i)","A-(i), B-(iv), C-(ii), D-(iii)","A-(iv), B-(iii), C-(i), D-(ii)"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"(A) With $u = -2f$, the lens formula gives $v = +2f$ and $m = -1$: real, inverted, same size, so (iii).\n(B) With $|u| < f$, $v$ comes out negative and $m > 1$: virtual, erect, magnified, so (iv).\n(C) For a concave lens and a real object, $v$ lies between $F$ and the lens and $0 < m < 1$, so (ii).\n(D) With $u = -f$, $\\frac{1}{v} = 0$, so the image is at infinity, (i).\nAnswer: (1)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Physics, Ch 9 · Ray Optics and Optical Instruments","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT12-PHY-RAY-OPTICS-080","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Ray Optics","qno":80,"subject":"Physics","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Ray Optics","type":"mcq","question":"A thin glass lens (refractive index 1.5) has a focal length of $+20$ cm in air. It is immersed completely in a transparent liquid of refractive index 1.6. What is its focal length in the liquid?","image":"","options":["$-160$ cm","$+160$ cm","$-80$ cm","$+64$ cm"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"Given: $n_g = 1.5$, $f_{air} = 20$ cm, $n_l = 1.6$.\nFormula: $\\frac{1}{f} = \\left(\\frac{n_2}{n_1} - 1\\right)K$, where $K = \\frac{1}{R_1} - \\frac{1}{R_2}$.\nIn air: $\\frac{1}{20} = 0.5K$, so $K = 0.1$ cm$^{-1}$.\nIn the liquid: $\\frac{1}{f} = \\left(\\frac{1.5}{1.6} - 1\\right)(0.1) = -0.00625$ cm$^{-1}$.\nSo f = $-160$ cm. The lens becomes diverging because the surrounding liquid is optically denser than the glass.\nAnswer: (1)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Physics, Ch 9 · Ray Optics and Optical Instruments","difficulty":"hard"},{"id":"NCERT12-PHY-RAY-OPTICS-081","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Ray Optics","qno":81,"subject":"Physics","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Ray Optics","type":"mcq","question":"An optician prescribes a corrective lens of power −2.0 D. The lens required is:","image":"","options":["a convex lens of focal length 50 cm","a concave lens of focal length 50 cm","a concave lens of focal length 20 cm","a convex lens of focal length 2 m"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["2"],"explanation":"Key fact: power is positive for a converging lens and negative for a diverging lens.\nA negative power therefore means a concave (diverging) lens.\nIts focal length is $f = \\frac{1}{P} = \\frac{1}{-2}$ m = −0.5 m = −50 cm.\nOption (1) has the right focal length but the wrong lens type. Option (3) wrongly takes f as 20 cm.\nAnswer: (2)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Physics, Ch 9 · Ray Optics and Optical Instruments","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT12-PHY-RAY-OPTICS-082","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Ray Optics","qno":82,"subject":"Physics","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Ray Optics","type":"mcq","question":"An equiconvex glass lens (refractive index 1.50) rests on a plane mirror. A needle moved along the principal axis above the lens coincides with its own inverted image when it is 30.0 cm from the lens. A few drops of a transparent liquid are now placed between the lens and the mirror, so the liquid forms a thin layer. The needle now coincides with its image at 50.0 cm from the lens. What is the refractive index of the liquid?","image":"","options":["1.40","1.33","1.60","1.25"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"The needle coincides with its image when rays leave the lens parallel, hit the mirror normally and retrace their path. So the needle is at the focus of the lens system.\nWithout liquid: $f = 30$ cm. Using $\\frac{1}{f} = (1.5 - 1)\\frac{2}{R}$ gives $R = 30$ cm.\nWith liquid, the combined focal length is 50 cm, so $\\frac{1}{f_l} = \\frac{1}{50} - \\frac{1}{30}$, giving $f_l = -75$ cm.\nThe liquid layer is a plano-concave lens of radius 30 cm, so $\\frac{1}{f_l} = -\\frac{\\mu - 1}{30}$.\nThis gives $\\mu - 1 = 0.4$, so $\\mu = 1.40$.\nAnswer: (1)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Physics, Ch 9 · Ray Optics and Optical Instruments","difficulty":"hard"},{"id":"NCERT12-PHY-RAY-OPTICS-083","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Ray Optics","qno":83,"subject":"Physics","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Ray Optics","type":"mcq","question":"A compound microscope has an objective of focal length 1.2 cm, an eyepiece of focal length 2.5 cm and a tube length of 18 cm. Find its magnifying power when the final image is at infinity. Take $D = 25$ cm.","image":"","options":["150","165","180","15"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"Given: $f_o = 1.2$ cm, $f_e = 2.5$ cm, $L = 18$ cm, $D = 25$ cm.\nFormula: $m = \\left(\\frac{L}{f_o}\\right)\\left(\\frac{D}{f_e}\\right)$\nSubstituting: $m_o = \\frac{18}{1.2} = 15$ and $m_e = \\frac{25}{2.5} = 10$\nSo m = 15 × 10 = 150. The value 165 wrongly uses $m_e = 1 + D/f_e = 11$, which is the near-point case.\nAnswer: (1)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Physics, Ch 9 · Ray Optics and Optical Instruments","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT12-PHY-RAY-OPTICS-084","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Ray Optics","qno":84,"subject":"Physics","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Ray Optics","type":"mcq","question":"Which of the following statements about the relation $\\frac{n_2}{v} - \\frac{n_1}{u} = \\frac{n_2 - n_1}{R}$ is INCORRECT?","image":"","options":["It assumes the aperture of the surface is small compared with the other distances involved","In its derivation, $n_1 \\sin i = n_2 \\sin r$ is approximated as $n_1 i = n_2 r$","It is valid only when light passes from a rarer to a denser medium ($n_2 > n_1$)","The normal at the point of incidence passes through the centre of curvature of the surface"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["3"],"explanation":"(1) Correct: the small aperture allows the small-angle approximation.\n(2) Correct: for paraxial rays, $\\sin\\theta \\approx \\tan\\theta \\approx \\theta$.\n(3) Incorrect: NCERT says the relation holds for any spherical surface. With proper signs it also works for $n_2 < n_1$, for example when an object inside glass is viewed from air.\n(4) Correct: the normal to a spherical surface lies along the radius.\nAnswer: (3)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Physics, Ch 9 · Ray Optics and Optical Instruments","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT12-PHY-RAY-OPTICS-085","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Ray Optics","qno":85,"subject":"Physics","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Ray Optics","type":"mcq","question":"Assertion (A): If the lower half of a concave mirror's reflecting surface is covered with opaque paint, the mirror still forms the image of the whole object, but the image is less bright.\nReason (R): The laws of reflection hold at every point of the uncovered part, so rays from each point of the object still reach the corresponding image point.","image":"","options":["Both A and R are true and R is the correct explanation of A","Both A and R are true but R is not the correct explanation of A","A is true but R is false","A is false but R is true"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"A is true. Covering half the mirror does not remove half the image. It only reduces the reflecting area, so less light reaches the image (about half the intensity).\nR is true. Every point of the uncovered half still reflects rays from every object point to the same image point.\nR is exactly why the full image survives, so R explains A.\nAnswer: (1)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Physics, Ch 9 · Ray Optics and Optical Instruments","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT12-PHY-RAY-OPTICS-086","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Ray Optics","qno":86,"subject":"Physics","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Ray Optics","type":"mcq","question":"A thin convex lens of power $+2.5$ D is placed in contact with a thin concave lens of power $-4.0$ D. What is the focal length of the combination?","image":"","options":["$-66.7$ cm","$+66.7$ cm","$-15.4$ cm","$+15.4$ cm"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"Given: $P_1 = +2.5$ D and $P_2 = -4.0$ D, with the lenses in contact.\nFormula: $P = P_1 + P_2$ (algebraic sum) and $f = \\frac{1}{P}$.\nSubstituting: $P = 2.5 - 4.0 = -1.5$ D, so $f = \\frac{1}{-1.5}$ m $= -0.667$ m.\nSo f = $-66.7$ cm. The negative sign shows the combination is diverging.\nAnswer: (1)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Physics, Ch 9 · Ray Optics and Optical Instruments","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT12-PHY-RAY-OPTICS-087","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Ray Optics","qno":87,"subject":"Physics","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Ray Optics","type":"mcq","question":"The refractive index of glass with respect to air is $\\frac{3}{2}$ and that of water with respect to air is $\\frac{4}{3}$. What is the refractive index of glass with respect to water?","image":"","options":["1.125","2.0","0.889","0.167"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"Given: $n_{ga} = \\frac{3}{2}$, $n_{wa} = \\frac{4}{3}$.\nFormula: $n_{32} = n_{31} \\times n_{12}$ and $n_{12} = \\frac{1}{n_{21}}$. Here this gives $n_{gw} = n_{ga} \\times n_{aw}$.\nSubstituting: $n_{aw} = \\frac{3}{4}$, so $n_{gw} = \\frac{3}{2} \\times \\frac{3}{4} = \\frac{9}{8}$.\nSo $n_{gw}$ = 1.125. The value 0.889 is the index of water with respect to glass.\nAnswer: (1)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Physics, Ch 9 · Ray Optics and Optical Instruments","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT12-PHY-RAY-OPTICS-088","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Ray Optics","qno":88,"subject":"Physics","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Ray Optics","type":"mcq","question":"Match List-I with List-II.\nList-I\n(a) Power of a lens\n(b) Magnifying power of a telescope in normal adjustment\n(c) Magnification of a simple microscope with the image at the near point\n(d) Focal length of a spherical mirror\nList-II\n(i) $\\frac{f_o}{f_e}$\n(ii) $\\frac{R}{2}$\n(iii) $\\frac{1}{f}$\n(iv) $1+\\frac{D}{f}$","image":"","options":["(a)-(i), (b)-(iii), (c)-(iv), (d)-(ii)","(a)-(iii), (b)-(i), (c)-(iv), (d)-(ii)","(a)-(iii), (b)-(i), (c)-(ii), (d)-(iv)","(a)-(iv), (b)-(i), (c)-(iii), (d)-(ii)"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["2"],"explanation":"(a) Power of a lens is $P = 1/f$, which is (iii).\n(b) A telescope in normal adjustment has $m = f_o/f_e$, which is (i).\n(c) A simple microscope with the image at the near point has $m = 1 + D/f$, which is (iv).\n(d) For paraxial rays, a spherical mirror has $f = R/2$, which is (ii).\nAnswer: (2)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Physics, Ch 9 · Ray Optics and Optical Instruments","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT12-PHY-RAY-OPTICS-089","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Ray Optics","qno":89,"subject":"Physics","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Ray Optics","type":"mcq","question":"Doctors use a bundle of optical fibres as a 'light pipe' to examine internal organs such as the oesophagus, stomach and intestines. The light can follow the bends of the pipe inside the body mainly because of:","image":"","options":["dispersion of light in the fibre","repeated total internal reflection inside each fibre","refraction through the fibre wall into the tissue","partial reflection at a silvered inner coating"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["2"],"explanation":"In each fibre, light strikes the inner surface at an angle larger than the critical angle and is totally reflected. This happens again and again along the length of the fibre.\nBecause of this, light travels along the fibre even when the fibre is bent. This is what lets the fibre act as an optical pipe.\nOption (4) is wrong: a silvered surface always absorbs or transmits some light, whereas TIR transmits none.\nAnswer: (2)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Physics, Ch 9 · Ray Optics and Optical Instruments","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT12-PHY-RAY-OPTICS-090","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Ray Optics","qno":90,"subject":"Physics","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Ray Optics","type":"mcq","question":"Assertion (A): A well-cut diamond sparkles far more brilliantly than a glass imitation of the same shape.\nReason (R): The critical angle of diamond is only about $24.4^\\circ$, so light entering it undergoes multiple total internal reflections before emerging.","image":"","options":["Both A and R are true, and R is the correct explanation of A","Both A and R are true, but R is not the correct explanation of A","A is true but R is false","A is false but R is true"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"R is true: diamond has n = 2.42, so $\\sin i_c = \\frac{1}{2.42}$, which gives $i_c \\approx 24.4^\\circ$.\nWith such a small critical angle, most rays striking the inner faces exceed $i_c$. They are totally reflected many times and finally emerge through a few faces.\nThis makes diamond brilliant, so A is true.\nNCERT cites multiple internal reflections in diamond as an example of TIR, so R correctly explains A.\nAnswer: (1)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Physics, Ch 9 · Ray Optics and Optical Instruments","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT12-PHY-RAY-OPTICS-091","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Ray Optics","qno":91,"subject":"Physics","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Ray Optics","type":"mcq","question":"A pin fixed on a table top is viewed from directly above. Two slabs with parallel faces are held horizontally above the pin, one on top of the other: a 6 cm thick glass slab (refractive index 1.5) and a 4 cm thick transparent plastic slab (refractive index $\\frac{4}{3}$). By how much does the pin appear to be raised? (Assume near-normal viewing.)","image":"","options":["3 cm","2 cm","7 cm","1 cm"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"Given: $t_1 = 6$ cm with $n_1 = 1.5$, and $t_2 = 4$ cm with $n_2 = \\frac{4}{3}$.\nFormula: each slab raises the object by $t\\left(1 - \\frac{1}{n}\\right)$. This shift does not depend on where the slab is placed, so the shifts from the two slabs simply add.\nSubstituting: shift from glass = $6\\left(1 - \\frac{2}{3}\\right) = 2$ cm; shift from plastic = $4\\left(1 - \\frac{3}{4}\\right) = 1$ cm.\nSo the total apparent rise = 3 cm. The value 7 cm is the combined apparent thickness of the slabs, not the shift.\nAnswer: (1)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Physics, Ch 9 · Ray Optics and Optical Instruments","difficulty":"hard"},{"id":"NCERT12-PHY-RAY-OPTICS-092","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Ray Optics","qno":92,"subject":"Physics","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Ray Optics","type":"mcq","question":"Statement I: A thick lens may form an image with coloured fringes when white light is used.\nStatement II: The refractive index of a medium does not depend on the wavelength of light.","image":"","options":["Both Statement I and Statement II are correct","Statement I is correct but Statement II is incorrect","Statement I is incorrect but Statement II is correct","Both Statement I and Statement II are incorrect"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["2"],"explanation":"Statement I is correct: NCERT notes that thick lenses give coloured images due to dispersion.\nStatement II is incorrect: NCERT states that $n_{21}$ depends on the wavelength of light.\nThis wavelength dependence is the cause of dispersion. Different colours are refracted by different amounts and focus at slightly different points.\nAnswer: (2)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Physics, Ch 9 · Ray Optics and Optical Instruments","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT12-PHY-RAY-OPTICS-093","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Ray Optics","qno":93,"subject":"Physics","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Ray Optics","type":"mcq","question":"A solid glass sphere of radius 10 cm and refractive index 1.5 contains a tiny air bubble 4 cm from its centre. The bubble is viewed from outside along the diameter through it, from the side nearer to the bubble. How far below the nearest point of the sphere's surface does the bubble appear?","image":"","options":["5 cm","4 cm","6 cm","7.5 cm"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"Given: the bubble is $10 - 4 = 6$ cm from the near surface. Light goes from glass to air, so $n_1 = 1.5$, $n_2 = 1$, $u = -6$ cm.\nThe centre of curvature is on the glass (incident) side, so $R = -10$ cm.\nFormula: $\\frac{n_2}{v} - \\frac{n_1}{u} = \\frac{n_2 - n_1}{R}$\nSubstituting: $\\frac{1}{v} + \\frac{1.5}{6} = \\frac{-0.5}{-10} = 0.05$, so $\\frac{1}{v} = -0.2$ and $v = -5$ cm.\nThe bubble appears 5 cm inside the surface (a virtual image).\nAnswer: (1)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Physics, Ch 9 · Ray Optics and Optical Instruments","difficulty":"hard"},{"id":"NCERT12-PHY-RAY-OPTICS-094","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Ray Optics","qno":94,"subject":"Physics","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Ray Optics","type":"mcq","question":"A 50 m tall chimney stands 2.5 km away from a telescope. The objective of the telescope has a focal length of 125 cm. What is the height of the image of the chimney formed by the objective?","image":"","options":["2.5 cm","0.4 cm","25 cm","1.25 cm"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"Given: h = 50 m, distance d = 2500 m, $f_o$ = 125 cm.\nThe chimney is very far away, so the objective forms its image at the focal plane. The angle subtended is $\\alpha = \\frac{h}{d} = \\frac{50}{2500} = 0.02$ rad.\nFormula: image height $h' = f_o \\alpha$\nSubstituting: $h' = 125 \\times 0.02$\nSo h' = 2.5 cm.\nAnswer: (1)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Physics, Ch 9 · Ray Optics and Optical Instruments","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT12-PHY-RAY-OPTICS-095","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Ray Optics","qno":95,"subject":"Physics","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Ray Optics","type":"mcq","question":"Which of the following pairs of conditions must both be satisfied for total internal reflection to occur at the interface between two transparent media?","image":"","options":["Light goes from the optically rarer to the optically denser medium, and the angle of incidence exceeds the critical angle","Light goes from the optically denser to the optically rarer medium, and the angle of incidence exceeds the critical angle","Light goes from the optically denser to the optically rarer medium, and the angle of incidence is less than the critical angle","Light may go in either direction, provided the angle of incidence is exactly $90^\\circ$"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["2"],"explanation":"Going from denser to rarer, the ray bends away from the normal, so the angle of refraction r is larger than i.\nWhen i equals the critical angle $i_c$, r becomes $90^\\circ$. For $i > i_c$, Snell's law cannot be satisfied, so no refraction occurs and all the light is reflected.\nFrom rarer to denser, r < i always, so refraction is always possible and TIR never occurs. This rules out option (1).\nFor $i < i_c$, light is only partially reflected.\nAnswer: (2)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Physics, Ch 9 · Ray Optics and Optical Instruments","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT12-PHY-RAY-OPTICS-096","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Ray Optics","qno":96,"subject":"Physics","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Ray Optics","type":"mcq","question":"Turpentine and water are compared. Which of the following statements is correct?","image":"","options":["Turpentine has a lower mass density but a higher optical density than water","Turpentine has both a higher mass density and a higher optical density than water","Light travels faster in turpentine than in water","A ray going obliquely from water into turpentine bends away from the normal"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"(1) Correct: NCERT gives this as its example. Turpentine is lighter than water (lower mass density) but optically denser.\n(2) Incorrect: its mass density is lower than that of water.\n(3) Incorrect: in an optically denser medium light travels more slowly.\n(4) Incorrect: a ray entering an optically denser medium bends towards the normal.\nAnswer: (1)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Physics, Ch 9 · Ray Optics and Optical Instruments","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT12-PHY-RAY-OPTICS-097","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Ray Optics","qno":97,"subject":"Physics","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Ray Optics","type":"mcq","question":"A convex lens forms a sharp real image of a candle flame on a screen. The outer ring-shaped part of the lens is now covered with black paper, and only a small central portion is left open. What happens to the image on the screen?","image":"","options":["The complete image is still formed, but it is less bright","Only the central part of the flame is imaged","The image disappears completely","The image becomes brighter and larger"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"Every point of the object sends rays to all parts of the lens.\nThe rays that pass through the uncovered central part still meet at the same image point.\nSo the whole image is still formed at the same place.\nFewer rays now reach the screen, so the image is dimmer. This is the same idea as the half-covered mirror in NCERT Example 9.1.\nOption (2) is tempting but wrong, because no single part of the lens images only one part of the object.\nAnswer: (1)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Physics, Ch 9 · Ray Optics and Optical Instruments","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT12-PHY-RAY-OPTICS-098","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Ray Optics","qno":98,"subject":"Physics","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Ray Optics","type":"mcq","question":"Assertion (A): For a concave mirror, the relation $f = \\frac{R}{2}$ holds only for rays incident close to the pole and making small angles with the principal axis.\nReason (R): The derivation of $f = \\frac{R}{2}$ uses $\\tan\\theta \\approx \\theta$ and assumes that the foot of the perpendicular D practically coincides with the pole P.","image":"","options":["Both A and R are true and R is the correct explanation of A","Both A and R are true but R is not the correct explanation of A","A is true but R is false","A is false but R is true"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"A is true. The relation applies to paraxial rays. Rays far from the axis meet the axis at slightly different points.\nR is true. The derivation sets $\\tan\\theta \\approx \\theta$ and $\\tan 2\\theta \\approx 2\\theta$, and takes FD ≈ f and CD ≈ R because D lies close to P.\nThese small-angle approximations are exactly why the result is limited to paraxial rays. So R explains A.\nAnswer: (1)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Physics, Ch 9 · Ray Optics and Optical Instruments","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT12-PHY-RAY-OPTICS-099","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Ray Optics","qno":99,"subject":"Physics","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Ray Optics","type":"mcq","question":"A car's side-view convex mirror has a radius of curvature of 4 m. A motorcycle behind the car approaches it at a constant speed of 8 m/s. At what speed does the image of the motorcycle move when the motorcycle is 18 m from the mirror?","image":"","options":["0.08 m/s","0.8 m/s","0.1 m/s","0.008 m/s"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"Given: f = +R/2 = +2 m, u = −18 m, and object speed = 8 m/s.\nImage position: $v = \\frac{fu}{u-f} = \\frac{2(-18)}{-20} = 1.8$ m.\nDifferentiating the mirror equation with respect to time gives $\\frac{dv}{dt} = -\\frac{v^2}{u^2}\\frac{du}{dt}$.\nSubstituting: image speed = $\\left(\\frac{1.8}{18}\\right)^2 \\times 8 = 0.01 \\times 8$\nSo image speed = 0.08 m/s. As the motorcycle comes closer, v/u grows, so the image moves faster.\nAnswer: (1)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Physics, Ch 9 · Ray Optics and Optical Instruments","difficulty":"hard"},{"id":"NCERT12-PHY-RAY-OPTICS-100","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Ray Optics","qno":100,"subject":"Physics","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Ray Optics","type":"mcq","question":"Assertion (A): For thin lenses placed in contact, the net power of the combination is the algebraic sum of the powers of the individual lenses.\nReason (R): The total magnification produced by a combination of lenses is the sum of the magnifications produced by the individual lenses.","image":"","options":["Both A and R are true and R is the correct explanation of A","Both A and R are true but R is not the correct explanation of A","A is true but R is false","A is false but R is true"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["3"],"explanation":"A is true. Adding the lens equations of thin lenses in contact gives $\\frac{1}{f} = \\frac{1}{f_1} + \\frac{1}{f_2} + \\ldots$, so $P = P_1 + P_2 + \\ldots$. Here convex lenses count as positive and concave lenses as negative.\nR is false. The image formed by one lens acts as the object for the next lens.\nSo the magnifications multiply: $m = m_1 m_2 m_3 \\ldots$. They do not add.\nAnswer: (3)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Physics, Ch 9 · Ray Optics and Optical Instruments","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT12-PHY-RAY-OPTICS-101","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Ray Optics","qno":101,"subject":"Physics","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Ray Optics","type":"mcq","question":"Assertion (A): For a simple magnifier, the angle subtended at the eye by the virtual image equals the angle subtended at the eye by the object itself, yet the magnifier provides angular magnification.\nReason (R): The magnifier lets the small object be placed much closer to the eye than 25 cm, so it subtends a larger angle than it would at the near point while its image remains comfortably viewable.","image":"","options":["Both A and R are true and R is the correct explanation of A","Both A and R are true but R is not the correct explanation of A","A is true but R is false","A is false but R is true"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"A is true. The image and the object lie along the same lines through the lens (eye close to lens), so they subtend equal angles at the eye.\nR is true. Without the lens, the object must be held at about 25 cm, where it subtends a tiny angle. With the lens, it can be kept near the focus, much closer, while the image stays at 25 cm or beyond.\nThe comparison is with the angle at D, not with the actual object angle. So R correctly explains A.\nAnswer: (1)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Physics, Ch 9 · Ray Optics and Optical Instruments","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT12-PHY-RAY-OPTICS-102","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Ray Optics","qno":102,"subject":"Physics","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Ray Optics","type":"mcq","question":"A point source of light lies at the bottom of a pond 28 cm deep (refractive index of water = $\\frac{4}{3}$). An opaque circular disc is floated on the water surface, centred directly above the source, so that no light from the source escapes into the air. What is the minimum radius of the disc?","image":"","options":["31.7 cm","21.0 cm","24.7 cm","18.5 cm"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"Given: h = 28 cm and $\\sin i_c = \\frac{3}{4}$, so $\\cos i_c = \\frac{\\sqrt{7}}{4}$ and $\\tan i_c = \\frac{3}{\\sqrt{7}} \\approx 1.134$.\nRays reaching the surface beyond radius $h\\tan i_c$ are totally reflected. Only rays within this circle can escape, so the disc must cover exactly this circle.\nFormula: $r = h\\tan i_c$\nSubstituting: $r = 28 \\times 1.134$\nSo r ≈ 31.7 cm.\nAnswer: (1)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Physics, Ch 9 · Ray Optics and Optical Instruments","difficulty":"hard"},{"id":"NCERT12-PHY-RAY-OPTICS-103","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Ray Optics","qno":103,"subject":"Physics","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Ray Optics","type":"mcq","question":"A coin lies at the bottom of a tank filled with water to a depth of 12 cm. Refractive index of water $= \\frac{4}{3}$. When viewed from directly above (near-normal viewing), at what depth below the water surface does the coin appear to be?","image":"","options":["9 cm","16 cm","3 cm","12 cm"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"Given: real depth = 12 cm, $n = \\frac{4}{3}$\nFormula: apparent depth = $\\frac{\\text{real depth}}{n}$\nSubstituting: apparent depth = $12 \\times \\frac{3}{4}$\nSo the coin appears 9 cm below the surface, i.e. it looks raised by 3 cm. The value 3 cm is the shift, not the apparent depth.\nAnswer: (1)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Physics, Ch 9 · Ray Optics and Optical Instruments","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT12-PHY-RAY-OPTICS-104","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Ray Optics","qno":104,"subject":"Physics","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Ray Optics","type":"mcq","question":"Assertion (A): For a given prism, the angle of deviation stays the same if the angle of incidence and the angle of emergence are interchanged.\nReason (R): The path of a ray of light is reversible.","image":"","options":["Both A and R are true and R is the correct explanation of A","Both A and R are true but R is not the correct explanation of A","A is true but R is false","A is false but R is true"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"A is true: in $\\delta = i + e - A$, the angles $i$ and $e$ enter symmetrically. Interchanging them leaves $\\delta$ unchanged.\nR is true: a ray traced backwards follows the same path.\nNCERT links the two directly. A ray entering at angle $e$ retraces the original path and emerges at angle $i$, with the same deviation. So R explains A.\nAnswer: (1)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Physics, Ch 9 · Ray Optics and Optical Instruments","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT12-PHY-RAY-OPTICS-105","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Ray Optics","qno":105,"subject":"Physics","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Ray Optics","type":"mcq","question":"Assertion (A): A real point object in air, placed anywhere in front of a convex glass surface, always forms a real image inside the glass.\nReason (R): For light going from air into glass across a convex surface, $\\frac{n_2 - n_1}{R}$ is positive, so the surface converges light.","image":"","options":["Both A and R are true, and R is the correct explanation of A","Both A and R are true, but R is not the correct explanation of A","A is true but R is false","A is false but R is true"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["4"],"explanation":"R is true: $n_2 > n_1$ and $R > 0$, so $\\frac{n_2 - n_1}{R} > 0$ and the surface is converging.\nA is false: from $\\frac{n_2}{v} = \\frac{n_2 - n_1}{R} + \\frac{n_1}{u}$, the image is real only if $\\frac{n_1}{|u|} < \\frac{n_2 - n_1}{R}$.\nIf the object is closer than $\\frac{n_1 R}{n_2 - n_1}$, then $v$ is negative and the image is virtual. Example: $u = -10$ cm, $R = 20$ cm gives $v = -20$ cm.\nAnswer: (4)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Physics, Ch 9 · Ray Optics and Optical Instruments","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT12-PHY-RAY-OPTICS-106","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Ray Optics","qno":106,"subject":"Physics","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Ray Optics","type":"mcq","question":"A convex spherical surface separates air (incident side) from glass of refractive index 1.5. Its radius of curvature is 10 cm. Match the object distance in List-I with the image position in List-II.\nList-I:\n(a) $u = -\\infty$\n(b) $u = -20$ cm\n(c) $u = -40$ cm\n(d) $u = -10$ cm\nList-II:\n(i) $v = +30$ cm\n(ii) image at infinity\n(iii) $v = +60$ cm\n(iv) $v = -30$ cm","image":"","options":["(a)-(iii), (b)-(ii), (c)-(i), (d)-(iv)","(a)-(i), (b)-(ii), (c)-(iii), (d)-(iv)","(a)-(i), (b)-(iv), (c)-(iii), (d)-(ii)","(a)-(ii), (b)-(i), (c)-(iv), (d)-(iii)"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["2"],"explanation":"Use $\\frac{1.5}{v} = \\frac{0.5}{10} + \\frac{1}{u} = 0.05 + \\frac{1}{u}$.\n(a) $\\frac{1}{u} = 0$: $\\frac{1.5}{v} = 0.05$, so $v = +30$ cm → (i).\n(b) $\\frac{1.5}{v} = 0.05 - 0.05 = 0$, so $v = \\infty$ → (ii).\n(c) $\\frac{1.5}{v} = 0.05 - 0.025 = 0.025$, so $v = +60$ cm → (iii).\n(d) $\\frac{1.5}{v} = 0.05 - 0.1 = -0.05$, so $v = -30$ cm, a virtual image → (iv).\nAnswer: (2)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Physics, Ch 9 · Ray Optics and Optical Instruments","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT12-PHY-RAY-OPTICS-107","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Ray Optics","qno":107,"subject":"Physics","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Ray Optics","type":"mcq","question":"A thin rod of length 10 cm lies along the principal axis of a concave mirror of focal length 10 cm. Its nearer end is 15 cm from the pole and its farther end is 25 cm from the pole. What is the length of the image of the rod?","image":"","options":["$13.3$ cm","$10.0$ cm","$20.0$ cm","$46.7$ cm"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"Given: $f = -10$ cm. The rod's ends are at $u = -15$ cm and $u = -25$ cm.\nFormula: $\\frac{1}{v} = \\frac{1}{f} - \\frac{1}{u}$\nNear end: $\\frac{1}{v} = -\\frac{1}{10} + \\frac{1}{15} = -\\frac{1}{30}$, so $v = -30$ cm.\nFar end: $\\frac{1}{v} = -\\frac{1}{10} + \\frac{1}{25} = -\\frac{3}{50}$, so $v = -16.7$ cm.\nSo the image length is $30 - 16.7 = 13.3$ cm. The magnification differs at each point along the rod, so the image is distorted.\nAnswer: (1)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Physics, Ch 9 · Ray Optics and Optical Instruments","difficulty":"hard"},{"id":"NCERT12-PHY-RAY-OPTICS-108","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Ray Optics","qno":108,"subject":"Physics","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Ray Optics","type":"mcq","question":"A point object in air is placed 10 cm from a convex spherical surface of glass (refractive index 1.5, radius of curvature 20 cm). Light is incident from the air side. Using the Cartesian sign convention, what is the image distance $v$?","image":"","options":["$-20$ cm","$+20$ cm","$+60$ cm","$-30$ cm"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"Given: $n_1 = 1$, $n_2 = 1.5$, $u = -10$ cm, $R = +20$ cm.\nFormula: $\\frac{n_2}{v} = \\frac{n_2 - n_1}{R} + \\frac{n_1}{u}$\nSubstituting: $\\frac{1.5}{v} = 0.025 - 0.1 = -0.075$\nSo $v = -20$ cm.\nThe negative sign means the image lies on the air side. It is a virtual image, 20 cm from the surface.\nAnswer: (1)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Physics, Ch 9 · Ray Optics and Optical Instruments","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT12-PHY-RAY-OPTICS-109","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Ray Optics","qno":109,"subject":"Physics","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Ray Optics","type":"mcq","question":"Match each substance in List-I with its critical angle with respect to air in List-II.\nList-I: (P) Water  (Q) Crown glass  (R) Dense flint glass  (S) Diamond\nList-II: (1) $24.4^\\circ$  (2) $37.3^\\circ$  (3) $41.1^\\circ$  (4) $48.8^\\circ$","image":"","options":["P-4, Q-3, R-2, S-1","P-1, Q-2, R-3, S-4","P-4, Q-2, R-3, S-1","P-3, Q-4, R-1, S-2"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"Since $\\sin i_c = \\frac{1}{n}$, a larger refractive index gives a smaller critical angle.\nThe refractive indices in increasing order are: water 1.33 < crown glass 1.52 < dense flint glass 1.62 < diamond 2.42.\nSo the critical angles decrease in the same order: water $48.8^\\circ$, crown glass $41.1^\\circ$, dense flint glass $37.3^\\circ$, diamond $24.4^\\circ$.\nAnswer: (1)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Physics, Ch 9 · Ray Optics and Optical Instruments","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT12-PHY-RAY-OPTICS-110","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Ray Optics","qno":110,"subject":"Physics","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Ray Optics","type":"mcq","question":"An equiconvex glass lens (n = 1.5) rests on a horizontal plane mirror. A needle held on the principal axis above the lens coincides with its own inverted image when it is 20 cm from the lens. A thin layer of liquid is then filled between the lens and the mirror. The needle now coincides with its image at 30 cm from the lens. What is the refractive index of the liquid?","image":"","options":["1.33","1.25","1.50","1.67"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"The needle coincides with its image only when the rays strike the mirror normally and retrace their path. This happens when the needle is at the focus of the lens system.\nGlass lens alone: f = 20 cm. Using $\\frac{1}{20} = 0.5 \\cdot \\frac{2}{R}$ gives R = 20 cm.\nThe liquid forms a plano-concave lens with radius 20 cm, so $\\frac{1}{f_l} = -\\frac{\\mu - 1}{20}$.\nFor lenses in contact: $\\frac{1}{30} = \\frac{1}{20} - \\frac{\\mu - 1}{20}$, which gives $\\mu - 1 = \\frac{1}{3}$.\nSo μ ≈ 1.33.\nAnswer: (1)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Physics, Ch 9 · Ray Optics and Optical Instruments","difficulty":"hard"},{"id":"NCERT12-PHY-RAY-OPTICS-111","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Ray Optics","qno":111,"subject":"Physics","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Ray Optics","type":"mcq","question":"An object and a screen are fixed a distance $D$ apart, and a convex lens of focal length $f$ is moved between them to form a real image on the screen. How many of the following statements are correct?\n(i) The smallest value of $D$ for which a real image can be formed is $4f$; at this separation the object is at $2f$ and $m = -1$.\n(ii) For $D > 4f$, there are two lens positions that give a sharp image on the screen.\n(iii) The product of the magnifications at these two positions equals 1.\n(iv) For $D < 4f$, a sharp real image can be obtained at exactly one lens position.","image":"","options":["1","2","3","4"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["3"],"explanation":"For a real image, $|u| + v = D$ with $\\frac{1}{v} + \\frac{1}{|u|} = \\frac{1}{f}$. This gives $|u|^2 - D|u| + Df = 0$.\n(i) Correct: real roots require $D^2 \\ge 4Df$, so $D \\ge 4f$. When $D = 4f$, the roots are equal, giving $|u| = v = 2f$ and $m = -1$.\n(ii) Correct: for $D > 4f$ there are two distinct roots, so two positions.\n(iii) Correct: $u$ and $v$ interchange between the positions, so $m_1 m_2 = \\frac{v}{u} \\cdot \\frac{u}{v} = 1$.\n(iv) Incorrect: for $D < 4f$ there are no real roots, so no position works.\nAnswer: (3)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Physics, Ch 9 · Ray Optics and Optical Instruments","difficulty":"hard"},{"id":"NCERT12-PHY-RAY-OPTICS-112","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Ray Optics","qno":112,"subject":"Physics","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Ray Optics","type":"mcq","question":"How many of the following statements about a compound microscope are correct?\n(i) For large magnification, both the objective and the eyepiece should have short focal lengths.\n(ii) The final image is erect with respect to the object.\n(iii) The first image is formed at or within the focal plane of the eyepiece.\n(iv) For the same objective magnification, the magnifying power is larger when the final image is at the near point than when it is at infinity.","image":"","options":["1","2","3","4"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["3"],"explanation":"(i) Correct: $m = (L/f_o)(D/f_e)$ increases as both $f_o$ and $f_e$ decrease.\n(ii) Incorrect: the objective inverts the image and the eyepiece does not re-invert it, so the final image is inverted.\n(iii) Correct: this is where the first image lies, so the eyepiece acts as a magnifier.\n(iv) Correct: $1 + D/f_e$ exceeds $D/f_e$ by 1.\nSo three statements are correct.\nAnswer: (3)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Physics, Ch 9 · Ray Optics and Optical Instruments","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT12-PHY-RAY-OPTICS-113","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Ray Optics","qno":113,"subject":"Physics","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Ray Optics","type":"mcq","question":"How many of the following statements about telescopes are correct?\n(i) In a Cassegrain telescope, a convex secondary mirror reflects the light so that it passes through a hole in the primary mirror.\n(ii) The resolving power of a telescope depends on the diameter of its objective.\n(iii) Using an eyepiece of longer focal length increases the magnifying power of the telescope.\n(iv) For a distant object, the objective of a refracting telescope forms a real image at its second focal point.\n(v) An astronomical telescope in normal adjustment produces an erect final image.\n(vi) A mirror is heavier than a lens of equivalent optical quality.","image":"","options":["2","3","4","5"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["2"],"explanation":"(i) Correct. This describes the Cassegrain design.\n(ii) Correct. Resolving power improves as the objective diameter increases.\n(iii) Incorrect. Since $m = \\frac{f_o}{f_e}$, a longer $f_e$ reduces the magnifying power.\n(iv) Correct. The objective forms a real image of a distant object at its second focal point.\n(v) Incorrect. The final image is inverted.\n(vi) Incorrect. A mirror weighs much less than an equivalent lens.\nSo 3 statements are correct.\nAnswer: (2)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Physics, Ch 9 · Ray Optics and Optical Instruments","difficulty":"hard"},{"id":"NCERT12-PHY-RAY-OPTICS-114","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Ray Optics","qno":114,"subject":"Physics","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Ray Optics","type":"mcq","question":"How many of the following statements about optical instruments are correct?\n(i) The objective of a compound microscope forms a real, inverted, magnified image.\n(ii) The final image in a compound microscope is erect with respect to the object.\n(iii) A telescope's objective has a larger focal length and a much larger aperture than its eyepiece.\n(iv) In normal adjustment, the length of a refracting telescope tube is $f_o + f_e$.\n(v) The resolving power of a telescope depends on the diameter of its objective.\n(vi) The light-gathering power of a telescope depends mainly on the focal length of its eyepiece.\n(vii) For a simple microscope, the magnification with the image at infinity is one more than that with the image at the near point.","image":"","options":["3","4","5","6"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["2"],"explanation":"(i) Correct: this is the job of the objective.\n(ii) Incorrect: the final image is inverted with respect to the object.\n(iii) Correct: the telescope objective has a large focal length and a large aperture.\n(iv) Correct: in normal adjustment, the tube length is $f_o + f_e$.\n(v) Correct: resolving power depends on the diameter of the objective.\n(vi) Incorrect: light-gathering power depends on the area of the objective, not the eyepiece.\n(vii) Incorrect: $D/f$ is one less than $1 + D/f$, not one more.\nThe correct statements are (i), (iii), (iv) and (v), so the count is 4.\nAnswer: (2)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Physics, Ch 9 · Ray Optics and Optical Instruments","difficulty":"hard"},{"id":"NCERT12-PHY-RAY-OPTICS-115","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Ray Optics","qno":115,"subject":"Physics","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Ray Optics","type":"mcq","question":"A person sits in a parked car and watches a jogger approach at constant speed in the convex side-view mirror.\nStatement I: The image of the jogger appears to move faster and faster as the jogger comes closer to the mirror.\nStatement II: For a convex mirror, $v = \\frac{fu}{u-f}$, and the change in $v$ for a given change in $u$ grows as $|u|$ decreases.","image":"","options":["Both Statement I and Statement II are correct","Both Statement I and Statement II are incorrect","Statement I is correct but Statement II is incorrect","Statement I is incorrect but Statement II is correct"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"Statement I is correct. In the NCERT example with $f = 1$ m, the image speed rises from $\\frac{1}{280}$ m/s at 39 m to $\\frac{1}{10}$ m/s at 9 m.\nStatement II is correct. The mirror equation gives $v = \\frac{fu}{u-f}$. When the object is far away, $v$ stays almost equal to $f$. Near the mirror, $v$ changes rapidly with $u$.\nSo both statements are correct, and II accounts for I.\nAnswer: (1)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Physics, Ch 9 · Ray Optics and Optical Instruments","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT12-PHY-RAY-OPTICS-116","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Ray Optics","qno":116,"subject":"Physics","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Ray Optics","type":"mcq","question":"An optical fibre consists of a central core surrounded by a cladding. For light to be guided along the fibre by total internal reflection, the refractive index of the core must be:","image":"","options":["higher than that of the cladding","lower than that of the cladding","equal to that of the cladding","equal to that of air"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"TIR can occur only when light travels from an optically denser medium towards a rarer one.\nInside the fibre, light strikes the core–cladding boundary from the core side. So the core must be denser, i.e., $n_{core} > n_{cladding}$.\nOption (2) fails because light going from a rarer to a denser medium bends towards the normal and can never be totally reflected.\nAnswer: (1)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Physics, Ch 9 · Ray Optics and Optical Instruments","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT12-PHY-RAY-OPTICS-117","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Ray Optics","qno":117,"subject":"Physics","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Ray Optics","type":"mcq","question":"How many of the following statements about ray tracing for spherical mirrors are correct?\n(i) A ray parallel to the principal axis passes through the focus after reflection from a concave mirror.\n(ii) A ray directed towards the centre of curvature of a convex mirror retraces its path after reflection.\n(iii) A ray passing through the focus of a concave mirror passes back through the focus after reflection.\n(iv) A ray incident obliquely at the pole is reflected so that the incident and reflected rays make equal angles with the principal axis.","image":"","options":["1","2","3","4"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["3"],"explanation":"(i) Correct. This is the definition of the principal focus for paraxial rays.\n(ii) Correct. The ray travels along the normal (the radius), so it reflects back on itself.\n(iii) Incorrect. A ray through F emerges parallel to the principal axis; it does not return through F.\n(iv) Correct. At the pole the normal is the principal axis, so the law of reflection gives equal angles on either side of it.\nThree statements are correct.\nAnswer: (3)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Physics, Ch 9 · Ray Optics and Optical Instruments","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT12-PHY-RAY-OPTICS-118","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Ray Optics","qno":118,"subject":"Physics","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Ray Optics","type":"mcq","question":"Statement I: A concave mirror with a focal length of 20 cm in air still has a focal length of 20 cm when completely immersed in water ($n = 1.33$).\nStatement II: The result $f = \\frac{R}{2}$ follows from the law of reflection and the geometry of the mirror alone, and the law of reflection does not involve the refractive index of the surrounding medium.","image":"","options":["Both statements are true and Statement II correctly explains Statement I","Both statements are true but Statement II does not explain Statement I","Statement I is true but Statement II is false","Statement I is false but Statement II is true"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"Statement II is true. The derivation uses only the angle of incidence equalling the angle of reflection (giving angle MCP = θ and angle MFP = 2θ) and small-angle geometry. No refractive index appears anywhere.\nStatement I is therefore true. The focal length depends only on R, which does not change in water.\nA lens behaves differently: its focal length changes in water because refraction depends on refractive index.\nStatement II directly explains Statement I.\nAnswer: (1)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Physics, Ch 9 · Ray Optics and Optical Instruments","difficulty":"hard"},{"id":"NCERT12-PHY-RAY-OPTICS-119","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Ray Optics","qno":119,"subject":"Physics","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Ray Optics","type":"mcq","question":"A small bulb rests at the bottom of a tank filled to a depth of 40 cm with a transparent liquid of refractive index $\\frac{5}{3}$. Treat the bulb as a point source. What is the area of the liquid surface through which light from the bulb can emerge into the air?","image":"","options":["0.283 $m^2$","0.503 $m^2$","0.181 $m^2$","0.893 $m^2$"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"Given: h = 0.40 m and n = $\\frac{5}{3}$, so $\\sin i_c = \\frac{3}{5}$ and $\\tan i_c = \\frac{3}{4}$.\nFormula: light escapes only within a circle of radius $r = h\\tan i_c$. Beyond this circle, rays strike the surface at more than $i_c$ and are totally reflected.\nSubstituting: $r = 0.40 \\times 0.75 = 0.30$ m, so $A = \\pi r^2 = \\pi (0.30)^2$.\nSo A ≈ 0.283 $m^2$.\nAnswer: (1)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Physics, Ch 9 · Ray Optics and Optical Instruments","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT12-PHY-RAY-OPTICS-120","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Ray Optics","qno":120,"subject":"Physics","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Ray Optics","type":"mcq","question":"Assertion (A): The magnifying power of a compound microscope increases if an objective of larger focal length is used, keeping the tube length and the eyepiece the same.\nReason (R): For the final image at infinity, the magnifying power of a compound microscope is $m = \\left(\\frac{L}{f_o}\\right)\\left(\\frac{D}{f_e}\\right)$.","image":"","options":["Both A and R are true and R is the correct explanation of A","Both A and R are true but R is not the correct explanation of A","A is true but R is false","A is false but R is true"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["4"],"explanation":"R is true. With the final image at infinity, $m = m_o m_e = (L/f_o)(D/f_e)$.\nA is false. Since $m \\propto 1/f_o$, a larger $f_o$ lowers the magnification. This is why both the objective and the eyepiece of a microscope need short focal lengths.\nAnswer: (4)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Physics, Ch 9 · Ray Optics and Optical Instruments","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT12-PHY-RAY-OPTICS-121","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Ray Optics","qno":121,"subject":"Physics","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Ray Optics","type":"mcq","question":"An object is placed in front of a concave mirror, and a real image is obtained on a screen. The lower half of the mirror's reflecting surface is now covered with a non-reflective black paint. What happens to the image on the screen?","image":"","options":["Only the upper half of the object is seen in the image","Only the lower half of the object is seen in the image","The complete image is formed, but its intensity is reduced","No image is formed because the principal axis is blocked"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["3"],"explanation":"Every point of the object sends rays to all parts of the mirror.\nThe uncovered half still obeys the laws of reflection at every point. So rays from each object point still meet at the corresponding image point.\nHence the whole object is imaged.\nOnly the reflecting area is halved, so less light reaches each image point and the image is dimmer.\nThe tempting option (1) wrongly assumes that each half of the mirror images only one half of the object.\nAnswer: (3)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Physics, Ch 9 · Ray Optics and Optical Instruments","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT12-PHY-RAY-OPTICS-122","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Ray Optics","qno":122,"subject":"Physics","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Ray Optics","type":"mcq","question":"A graph of the angle of deviation $\\delta$ against the angle of incidence $i$ is drawn for a triangular prism. A value of $\\delta$ that is greater than the minimum deviation $D_m$ (and within the range shown on the graph) corresponds to:","image":"","options":["exactly one value of $i$","two different values of $i$","infinitely many values of $i$","no value of $i$"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["2"],"explanation":"The $\\delta$–$i$ curve is U-shaped and has its minimum at $D_m$, where $i = e$.\nIn $\\delta = i + e - A$, the angles $i$ and $e$ appear symmetrically. Swapping them gives the same $\\delta$.\nSo every $\\delta$ above $D_m$ is produced by two angles of incidence: one equal to $i$ and the other equal to the corresponding $e$.\nOnly $\\delta = D_m$ corresponds to a single $i$, so option (1) is wrong.\nAnswer: (2)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Physics, Ch 9 · Ray Optics and Optical Instruments","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT12-PHY-RAY-OPTICS-123","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Ray Optics","qno":123,"subject":"Physics","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Ray Optics","type":"mcq","question":"Assertion (A): Turpentine is optically denser than water, even though its mass density is less than that of water.\nReason (R): Optical density compares the speed of light in two media and is not the same as mass per unit volume.","image":"","options":["Both A and R are true and R is the correct explanation of A","Both A and R are true but R is not the correct explanation of A","A is true but R is false","A is false but R is true"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"A is true. NCERT gives turpentine and water as an example: turpentine has the lower mass density but the higher optical density.\nR is true. Optical density depends on the ratio of the speeds of light in the two media. Mass density is mass per unit volume.\nBecause the two quantities measure different things, one medium can be optically denser while being lighter. So R correctly explains A.\nAnswer: (1)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Physics, Ch 9 · Ray Optics and Optical Instruments","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT12-PHY-RAY-OPTICS-124","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Ray Optics","qno":124,"subject":"Physics","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Ray Optics","type":"mcq","question":"A right-angled isosceles prism of crown glass (n = 1.52) bends a ray by $90^\\circ$ through total internal reflection at its hypotenuse face, where the ray is incident at $45^\\circ$. The whole prism is now immersed in water (n = 1.33). Which statement correctly describes the result?","image":"","options":["The prism still bends the ray by $90^\\circ$, because glass remains denser than water","Total internal reflection fails, because the critical angle becomes about $61^\\circ$, which is greater than $45^\\circ$","Total internal reflection fails, because the critical angle becomes about $41^\\circ$","The prism works even better, because the critical angle drops to about $24^\\circ$"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["2"],"explanation":"In air: $\\sin i_c = \\frac{1}{1.52}$, so $i_c \\approx 41.1^\\circ$. Since $45^\\circ > 41.1^\\circ$, TIR occurs.\nIn water: $\\sin i_c = \\frac{1.33}{1.52} = 0.875$, so $i_c \\approx 61^\\circ$.\nThe angle of incidence ($45^\\circ$) is now below $i_c$. Most of the light refracts into the water, and only partial reflection remains.\nGlass being denser than water is necessary for TIR but not sufficient, so option (1) fails.\nAnswer: (2)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Physics, Ch 9 · Ray Optics and Optical Instruments","difficulty":"hard"},{"id":"NCERT12-PHY-RAY-OPTICS-125","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Ray Optics","qno":125,"subject":"Physics","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Ray Optics","type":"mcq","question":"Treat a flat water surface as a spherical surface with $R \\to \\infty$. A coin lies 12 cm below the surface of water (refractive index $\\frac{4}{3}$) and is viewed from air nearly along the normal. Using $\\frac{n_2}{v} - \\frac{n_1}{u} = \\frac{n_2 - n_1}{R}$, how far below the surface does the coin appear?","image":"","options":["9 cm","16 cm","12 cm","6 cm"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"Given: light travels from water to air, so $n_1 = \\frac{4}{3}$, $n_2 = 1$, $u = -12$ cm, $R \\to \\infty$.\nFormula: with $R \\to \\infty$ the right side is zero, so $\\frac{n_2}{v} = \\frac{n_1}{u}$.\nSubstituting: $\\frac{1}{v} = \\frac{4/3}{-12} = -\\frac{1}{9}$, so $v = -9$ cm.\nThe image is virtual, 9 cm below the surface. This matches apparent depth = real depth / $n$.\nAnswer: (1)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Physics, Ch 9 · Ray Optics and Optical Instruments","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT12-PHY-RAY-OPTICS-126","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Ray Optics","qno":126,"subject":"Physics","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Ray Optics","type":"mcq","question":"Assertion (A): A reflecting telescope with a concave mirror objective forms images free of chromatic aberration.\nReason (R): A mirror can be supported over its entire back surface, whereas a large lens can be supported only by its rim.","image":"","options":["Both A and R are true and R is the correct explanation of A","Both A and R are true but R is not the correct explanation of A","A is true but R is false","A is false but R is true"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["2"],"explanation":"A is true. Reflection does not depend on wavelength, so a mirror produces no chromatic aberration.\nR is true. NCERT notes that a mirror can be supported over its whole back surface, while a lens is held only by its edge.\nHowever, R explains why mechanical support is easier for a mirror. It says nothing about why colour defects are absent, so R does not explain A.\nAnswer: (2)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Physics, Ch 9 · Ray Optics and Optical Instruments","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT12-PHY-RAY-OPTICS-127","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Ray Optics","qno":127,"subject":"Physics","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Ray Optics","type":"mcq","question":"A ray passes through an equilateral glass prism (refracting angle 60°) in the position of minimum deviation. The angle of incidence on the first face is 50°. What is the refractive index of the prism material?","image":"","options":["1.53","1.41","1.73","1.62"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"Given: $A = 60°$ and $i = 50°$ at minimum deviation, where $i = e$.\nFormula: $D_m = 2i - A$ and $n = \\frac{\\sin[(A+D_m)/2]}{\\sin(A/2)}$.\nSubstituting: $D_m = 100° - 60° = 40°$, so $n = \\frac{\\sin 50°}{\\sin 30°} = \\frac{0.766}{0.5}$.\nSo n ≈ 1.53. Inside the prism, $r_1 = r_2 = 30°$ and the ray is parallel to the base.\nAnswer: (1)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Physics, Ch 9 · Ray Optics and Optical Instruments","difficulty":"hard"},{"id":"NCERT12-PHY-RAY-OPTICS-128","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Ray Optics","qno":128,"subject":"Physics","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Ray Optics","type":"mcq","question":"A 3.0 cm tall object stands upright on the principal axis, 10 cm in front of a concave mirror of radius of curvature 40 cm. What is the height of the image, with its sign according to the Cartesian convention?","image":"","options":["$+6.0$ cm","$-6.0$ cm","$+3.0$ cm","$-1.5$ cm"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"Given: $f = -20$ cm, $u = -10$ cm, $h = +3.0$ cm.\nFormula: $\\frac{1}{v} = \\frac{1}{f} - \\frac{1}{u}$ and $m = -\\frac{v}{u} = \\frac{h'}{h}$\nSubstituting: $\\frac{1}{v} = -\\frac{1}{20} + \\frac{1}{10} = \\frac{1}{20}$, so $v = +20$ cm. Then $m = -\\frac{20}{-10} = +2$.\nSo $h' = 2 \\times 3.0 = +6.0$ cm. The image is virtual, erect and magnified, as expected for an object between P and F.\nAnswer: (1)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Physics, Ch 9 · Ray Optics and Optical Instruments","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT12-PHY-RAY-OPTICS-129","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Ray Optics","qno":129,"subject":"Physics","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Ray Optics","type":"mcq","question":"Medium 1 is air, medium 2 is water and medium 3 is glass. The refractive index of water with respect to air is $n_{21} = \\frac{4}{3}$, and that of glass with respect to air is $n_{31} = \\frac{3}{2}$. What is the refractive index of glass with respect to water, $n_{32}$?","image":"","options":["1.125","2.0","0.889","0.5"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"Given: $n_{21} = \\frac{4}{3}$, $n_{31} = \\frac{3}{2}$\nFormula: $n_{32} = n_{31} \\times n_{12}$, where $n_{12} = \\frac{1}{n_{21}} = \\frac{3}{4}$\nSubstituting: $n_{32} = \\frac{3}{2} \\times \\frac{3}{4} = \\frac{9}{8}$\nSo $n_{32} = 1.125$. The value 2.0 comes from wrongly multiplying $n_{31}$ by $n_{21}$, and 0.889 is the inverse ratio.\nAnswer: (1)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Physics, Ch 9 · Ray Optics and Optical Instruments","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT12-PHY-RAY-OPTICS-130","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Ray Optics","qno":130,"subject":"Physics","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Ray Optics","type":"mcq","question":"A thin double convex lens is made of glass of refractive index 1.6. Its two faces have radii of curvature 20 cm and 30 cm. What is its focal length in air?","image":"","options":["20 cm","12 cm","10 cm","40 cm"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"Given: n = 1.6, R1 = +20 cm, R2 = –30 cm (Cartesian sign convention for a double convex lens).\nFormula: $\\frac{1}{f} = (n-1)\\left(\\frac{1}{R_1} - \\frac{1}{R_2}\\right)$\nSubstituting: $\\frac{1}{f} = 0.6\\left(\\frac{1}{20} + \\frac{1}{30}\\right) = 0.6 \\times \\frac{5}{60} = 0.05$ cm$^{-1}$\nSo f = 20 cm.\nAnswer: (1)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Physics, Ch 9 · Ray Optics and Optical Instruments","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT12-PHY-RAY-OPTICS-131","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Ray Optics","qno":131,"subject":"Physics","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Ray Optics","type":"mcq","question":"A ray of light passes through a prism of refracting angle $60^\\circ$. The angle of incidence on the first face is $50^\\circ$ and the angle of emergence from the second face is $40^\\circ$. What is the angle of deviation produced by the prism?","image":"","options":["$30^\\circ$","$90^\\circ$","$10^\\circ$","$50^\\circ$"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"Given: $A = 60^\\circ$, $i = 50^\\circ$, $e = 40^\\circ$.\nFormula: $\\delta = i + e - A$.\nSubstituting: $\\delta = 50^\\circ + 40^\\circ - 60^\\circ$.\nSo $\\delta = 30^\\circ$.\nOption (2), $90^\\circ$, comes from adding $i$ and $e$ and forgetting to subtract $A$.\nAnswer: (1)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Physics, Ch 9 · Ray Optics and Optical Instruments","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT12-PHY-RAY-OPTICS-132","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Ray Optics","qno":132,"subject":"Physics","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Ray Optics","type":"mcq","question":"Light travelling in crown glass (n = 1.50) strikes a plane glass–water interface, where water has n = 1.33. What is the critical angle for this interface?","image":"","options":["$62.5^\\circ$","$41.8^\\circ$","$48.8^\\circ$","$27.5^\\circ$"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"Given: the denser medium is glass ($n_1$ = 1.50) and the rarer medium is water ($n_2$ = 1.33).\nFormula: $\\sin i_c = n_{21} = \\frac{n_2}{n_1}$\nSubstituting: $\\sin i_c = \\frac{1.33}{1.50} = 0.887$\nSo $i_c \\approx 62.5^\\circ$. Option (2), $41.8^\\circ$, is the glass–air value, which wrongly ignores the water.\nAnswer: (1)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Physics, Ch 9 · Ray Optics and Optical Instruments","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT12-PHY-RAY-OPTICS-133","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Ray Optics","qno":133,"subject":"Physics","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Ray Optics","type":"mcq","question":"A coin lies at the bottom of a tank filled with water to a depth of 2.4 m. The refractive index of water is $\\frac{4}{3}$. When viewed from directly above (near-normal viewing), at what depth below the water surface does the coin appear to be?","image":"","options":["1.8 m","3.2 m","0.6 m","2.4 m"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"Given: real depth = 2.4 m, $n = \\frac{4}{3}$, viewing near the normal.\nFormula: apparent depth = $\\frac{\\text{real depth}}{n}$.\nSubstituting: apparent depth = $2.4 \\times \\frac{3}{4}$.\nSo the apparent depth = 1.8 m. The coin appears raised by 0.6 m, which is why 0.6 m is a tempting wrong option.\nAnswer: (1)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Physics, Ch 9 · Ray Optics and Optical Instruments","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT12-PHY-RAY-OPTICS-134","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Ray Optics","qno":134,"subject":"Physics","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Ray Optics","type":"mcq","question":"In a two-lens system, the image formed by the first lens acts as the object for the second lens. The first lens alone produces a magnification of −3 and the second produces −0.5. Which option correctly describes the final image relative to the original object?","image":"","options":["Magnification −3.5; inverted","Magnification +1.5; erect and enlarged","Magnification −1.5; inverted and enlarged","Magnification +6; erect and enlarged"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["2"],"explanation":"Key fact: for a lens combination, the total magnification is the product of the individual magnifications, $m = m_1 m_2$.\nSubstituting: m = (−3)(−0.5) = +1.5.\nA positive m means the final image is erect with respect to the object. Since |m| > 1, it is also enlarged.\nOption (1) wrongly adds the magnifications instead of multiplying them.\nAnswer: (2)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Physics, Ch 9 · Ray Optics and Optical Instruments","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT12-PHY-RAY-OPTICS-135","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Ray Optics","qno":135,"subject":"Physics","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Ray Optics","type":"mcq","question":"Statement I: For a parallel-sided glass slab in air, the lateral shift of an obliquely incident ray increases if the slab is made thicker or if the angle of incidence is increased.\nStatement II: When a pin on a table is viewed from above through a glass slab held parallel to the table, the apparent rise of the pin is greater when the slab is held closer to the eye than when it lies near the pin.","image":"","options":["Both Statement I and Statement II are correct","Both Statement I and Statement II are incorrect","Statement I is correct but Statement II is incorrect","Statement I is incorrect but Statement II is correct"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["3"],"explanation":"Statement I correct: the lateral shift is $d = \\frac{t \\sin(i - r)}{\\cos r}$.\nThis grows with the thickness t. As i increases, $(i - r)$ increases and $\\cos r$ decreases, so d also grows.\nStatement II incorrect: for near-normal viewing, the apparent rise is $t\\left(1 - \\frac{1}{n}\\right)$.\nThis depends only on the slab's thickness and refractive index, not on where the slab is placed between the pin and the eye.\nAnswer: (3)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Physics, Ch 9 · Ray Optics and Optical Instruments","difficulty":"hard"},{"id":"NCERT12-PHY-RAY-OPTICS-136","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Ray Optics","qno":136,"subject":"Physics","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Ray Optics","type":"mcq","question":"Match List-I (ray incident on a thin lens) with List-II (path after refraction).\nList-I\n(A) Ray passing through the optical centre\n(B) Ray parallel to the principal axis falling on a convex lens\n(C) Ray passing through the first principal focus of a convex lens\n(D) Ray parallel to the principal axis falling on a concave lens\nList-II\n(i) Emerges parallel to the principal axis\n(ii) Passes through the second principal focus F′\n(iii) Appears to diverge from the first principal focus F\n(iv) Emerges without deviation","image":"","options":["A-iv, B-ii, C-i, D-iii","A-iv, B-i, C-ii, D-iii","A-i, B-ii, C-iv, D-iii","A-iv, B-iii, C-i, D-ii"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"(A) A ray through the optical centre goes on undeviated, so A matches (iv).\n(B) For a convex lens, a ray parallel to the axis converges through the second focus F′, so B matches (ii).\n(C) A ray through the first focus of a convex lens comes out parallel to the axis, so C matches (i).\n(D) For a concave lens, a parallel ray appears to diverge from the first focus F, so D matches (iii).\nAnswer: (1)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Physics, Ch 9 · Ray Optics and Optical Instruments","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT12-PHY-RAY-OPTICS-137","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Ray Optics","qno":137,"subject":"Physics","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Ray Optics","type":"mcq","question":"A watchmaker views a tiny screw through a convex lens of focal length 5 cm. The screw is placed at the focus of the lens, so the image is at infinity. Taking $D = 25$ cm, what is the angular magnification?","image":"","options":["5","6","4","0.2"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"Given: $f = 5$ cm, image at infinity, $D = 25$ cm.\nFormula: $m = \\frac{\\theta_i}{\\theta_o} = \\frac{D}{f}$\nSubstituting: $m = \\frac{25}{5}$\nSo m = 5. The value 6 would be correct only if the image were at the near point, using $1 + D/f$.\nAnswer: (1)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Physics, Ch 9 · Ray Optics and Optical Instruments","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT12-PHY-RAY-OPTICS-138","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Ray Optics","qno":138,"subject":"Physics","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Ray Optics","type":"mcq","question":"A convex lens of focal length 6.25 cm is held close to the eye as a magnifier. At first the object is placed so that the virtual image forms at the near point (25 cm). The object is then shifted so that the image forms at infinity for relaxed viewing. By how much must the object be moved away from the lens?","image":"","options":["1.25 cm","0.75 cm","2.5 cm","5.0 cm"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"Given: f = 6.25 cm. Initially v = -25 cm.\nFormula: $\\frac{1}{v} - \\frac{1}{u} = \\frac{1}{f}$\nSubstituting: $\\frac{1}{u} = -\\frac{1}{25} - \\frac{1}{6.25} = -0.2$, so u = -5 cm.\nFor an image at infinity, the object must sit at the focus: |u| = 6.25 cm.\nSo the shift = 6.25 - 5 = 1.25 cm away from the lens. The magnifying power drops from 5 to 4.\nAnswer: (1)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Physics, Ch 9 · Ray Optics and Optical Instruments","difficulty":"hard"},{"id":"NCERT12-PHY-RAY-OPTICS-139","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Ray Optics","qno":139,"subject":"Physics","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Ray Optics","type":"mcq","question":"A right-angled isosceles prism ($45^\\circ$–$90^\\circ$–$45^\\circ$) is kept in air. Light falls normally on one of its shorter faces, and the prism is meant to turn the light through $90^\\circ$ by total internal reflection at the hypotenuse. A prism made of which of the following materials would NOT work? (Refractive indices: water 1.33, crown glass 1.52, dense flint glass 1.62, diamond 2.42)","image":"","options":["Crown glass","Dense flint glass","Diamond","Water (in a thin transparent shell of negligible effect)"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["4"],"explanation":"The ray enters normally, so it meets the hypotenuse at $45^\\circ$. TIR needs $i_c < 45^\\circ$.\nCritical angles from NCERT Table 9.1: crown glass 41.14°, dense flint glass 37.31°, diamond 24.41°. All are below 45°, so TIR occurs.\nWater has $i_c$ = 48.75°, which is greater than 45°. The ray would be partly refracted out of the hypotenuse.\nAnswer: (4)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Physics, Ch 9 · Ray Optics and Optical Instruments","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT12-PHY-RAY-OPTICS-140","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Ray Optics","qno":140,"subject":"Physics","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Ray Optics","type":"mcq","question":"An astronomical telescope has a tube length of 105 cm and a magnifying power of 20 in normal adjustment. The eyepiece is then moved so that the final image of a distant star forms at 25 cm from the eyepiece. What is the new separation between the objective and the eyepiece?","image":"","options":["105.0 cm","104.2 cm","105.8 cm","125.0 cm"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["2"],"explanation":"Given: $f_o + f_e$ = 105 cm and $\\frac{f_o}{f_e}$ = 20. Solving these gives $f_o$ = 100 cm and $f_e$ = 5 cm.\nThe objective's image stays at 100 cm from the objective.\nFor the eyepiece, take v = −25 cm and f = +5 cm. Then $\\frac{1}{u} = \\frac{1}{v} - \\frac{1}{f} = -\\frac{1}{25} - \\frac{1}{5} = -\\frac{6}{25}$, so u = −4.17 cm.\nSeparation = 100 + 4.17 = 104.2 cm.\nThe eyepiece moves slightly closer to the objective.\nAnswer: (2)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Physics, Ch 9 · Ray Optics and Optical Instruments","difficulty":"hard"},{"id":"NCERT12-PHY-RAY-OPTICS-141","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Ray Optics","qno":141,"subject":"Physics","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Ray Optics","type":"mcq","question":"A small refracting telescope has an objective of focal length 80 cm and an eyepiece of focal length 5 cm. What is the separation between the objective and the eyepiece in normal adjustment?","image":"","options":["75 cm","85 cm","16 cm","400 cm"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["2"],"explanation":"Given: $f_o$ = 80 cm, $f_e$ = 5 cm.\nIn normal adjustment, the objective forms the image at its second focal point. This point must also be the first focal point of the eyepiece.\nFormula: tube length $L = f_o + f_e$\nSubstituting: L = 80 + 5\nSo L = 85 cm.\n16 is $f_o/f_e$, which is the magnifying power, not a length.\nAnswer: (2)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Physics, Ch 9 · Ray Optics and Optical Instruments","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT12-PHY-RAY-OPTICS-142","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Ray Optics","qno":142,"subject":"Physics","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Ray Optics","type":"mcq","question":"A thin convex lens made of glass of refractive index 1.5 has a focal length of 20 cm in air. What is its focal length when it is completely immersed in a liquid of refractive index 1.25?","image":"","options":["25 cm","40 cm","50 cm","100 cm"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["3"],"explanation":"Given: $n_g = 1.5$, $f_{air} = 20$ cm, $n_l = 1.25$.\nFormula: $\\frac{1}{f} = \\frac{n_2-n_1}{n_1}\\left(\\frac{1}{R_1}-\\frac{1}{R_2}\\right)$.\nIn air: $\\frac{1}{20} = 0.5K$, which gives $K = 0.1\\ \\text{cm}^{-1}$.\nIn the liquid: $\\frac{1.5-1.25}{1.25} = 0.2$, so $\\frac{1}{f} = 0.2 \\times 0.1 = 0.02\\ \\text{cm}^{-1}$.\nSo $f = 50$ cm. The lens remains converging.\nAnswer: (3)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Physics, Ch 9 · Ray Optics and Optical Instruments","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT12-PHY-RAY-OPTICS-143","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Ray Optics","qno":143,"subject":"Physics","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Ray Optics","type":"mcq","question":"Light is an electromagnetic wave, yet we treat it as travelling in straight lines (rays) for ordinary objects. The reason is that:","image":"","options":["the speed of light is extremely large","light waves are transverse","the wavelength of light is very small compared with the size of ordinary objects","light does not need a material medium to travel"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["3"],"explanation":"Visible wavelengths are a few hundred nanometres.\nOrdinary objects are a few centimetres or larger, so the wavelength is tiny by comparison.\nUnder this condition a light wave can be treated as moving along the straight line joining two points. This straight path is a ray, and a bundle of rays is a beam.\nThe large speed of light does not affect whether its path is straight, so option (1) is wrong.\nAnswer: (3)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Physics, Ch 9 · Ray Optics and Optical Instruments","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT12-PHY-RAY-OPTICS-144","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Ray Optics","qno":144,"subject":"Physics","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Ray Optics","type":"mcq","question":"Statement I: For a simple microscope, the magnification with the image at the near point is greater by one than the angular magnification with the image at infinity.\nStatement II: The final image formed by a compound microscope is erect with respect to the original object.","image":"","options":["Both Statement I and Statement II are true","Both Statement I and Statement II are false","Statement I is true but Statement II is false","Statement I is false but Statement II is true"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["3"],"explanation":"Statement I is true. With the image at the near point, $m = 1 + \\frac{D}{f}$. With the image at infinity, $m = \\frac{D}{f}$. The difference is exactly 1.\nStatement II is false. The objective forms a real, inverted image. The eyepiece magnifies this image without inverting it again, so the final image is inverted with respect to the object.\nAnswer: (3)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Physics, Ch 9 · Ray Optics and Optical Instruments","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT12-PHY-RAY-OPTICS-145","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Ray Optics","qno":145,"subject":"Physics","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Ray Optics","type":"mcq","question":"The refractive index of medium 2 with respect to medium 1 is $n_{21} = 1.5$. What is the refractive index of medium 1 with respect to medium 2, $n_{12}$?","image":"","options":["0.67","1.5","0.5","2.5"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"Given: $n_{21} = 1.5$\nFormula: $n_{12} = \\frac{1}{n_{21}}$\nSubstituting: $n_{12} = \\frac{1}{1.5}$\nSo $n_{12} = \\frac{2}{3} \\approx 0.67$. Reversing the pair of media gives the reciprocal, not the same value.\nAnswer: (1)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Physics, Ch 9 · Ray Optics and Optical Instruments","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT12-PHY-RAY-OPTICS-146","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Ray Optics","qno":146,"subject":"Physics","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Ray Optics","type":"mcq","question":"A thin prism has a refracting angle of $4^\\circ$ and is made of glass of refractive index 1.5. What is the deviation it produces for a ray in air?","image":"","options":["$2^\\circ$","$6^\\circ$","$4^\\circ$","$1^\\circ$"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"Given: $A = 4^\\circ$, $n = 1.5$ (thin prism).\nFormula: $D_m = (n - 1)A$.\nSubstituting: $D_m = (1.5 - 1) \\times 4^\\circ$.\nSo $D_m = 2^\\circ$.\nOption (2), $6^\\circ$, comes from using $nA$ instead of $(n-1)A$.\nAnswer: (1)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Physics, Ch 9 · Ray Optics and Optical Instruments","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT12-PHY-RAY-OPTICS-147","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Ray Optics","qno":147,"subject":"Physics","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Ray Optics","type":"mcq","question":"For a concave mirror, match the object position in List-I with the image description in List-II.\nList-I\n(a) Object beyond C\n(b) Object at C\n(c) Object between F and C\n(d) Object between P and F\nList-II\n(i) Beyond C, real, inverted, magnified\n(ii) Behind the mirror, virtual, erect, magnified\n(iii) Between F and C, real, inverted, diminished\n(iv) At C, real, inverted, same size","image":"","options":["(a)-(iii), (b)-(iv), (c)-(i), (d)-(ii)","(a)-(i), (b)-(iv), (c)-(iii), (d)-(ii)","(a)-(iii), (b)-(ii), (c)-(i), (d)-(iv)","(a)-(iv), (b)-(iii), (c)-(ii), (d)-(i)"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"(a) Object beyond C: the image forms between F and C and is real, inverted and diminished, so (iii).\n(b) Object at C (u = 2f): v = 2f and m = −1, so the image is at C and the same size, (iv).\n(c) Object between F and C: the image forms beyond C, real and magnified, so (i).\n(d) Object between P and F: v is positive, so the image is virtual, erect and magnified, (ii). Example 9.3(ii) shows this case.\nAnswer: (1)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Physics, Ch 9 · Ray Optics and Optical Instruments","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT12-PHY-RAY-OPTICS-148","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Ray Optics","qno":148,"subject":"Physics","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Ray Optics","type":"mcq","question":"A ray of light travelling in water ($n = 1.33$) strikes the water–air surface at an angle of incidence of $50^\\circ$. The critical angle for water–air is about $48.75^\\circ$. What happens to this ray at the surface?","image":"","options":["It refracts into air and grazes along the surface at $90^\\circ$","It is totally reflected back into the water, and no light enters the air","It partly refracts into air, bending towards the normal, and partly reflects","It passes into air undeviated"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["2"],"explanation":"The ray travels from a denser medium (water) to a rarer medium (air), which is the first condition for total internal reflection.\nIts angle of incidence, $50^\\circ$, is greater than the critical angle of $48.75^\\circ$, which is the second condition.\nWhen $i > i_c$, Snell's law cannot be satisfied, so no refracted ray exists and all the light is reflected back into the water.\nGrazing emergence along the surface happens only when $i = i_c$ exactly, so option (1) is wrong.\nAnswer: (2)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Physics, Ch 9 · Ray Optics and Optical Instruments","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT12-PHY-RAY-OPTICS-149","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Ray Optics","qno":149,"subject":"Physics","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Ray Optics","type":"mcq","question":"Statement I: A totally reflecting glass prism used in place of a silvered plane mirror in an optical instrument gives a brighter image.\nStatement II: In total internal reflection, a small fraction of the incident light is always transmitted into the rarer medium.","image":"","options":["Both Statement I and Statement II are correct","Statement I is correct but Statement II is incorrect","Statement I is incorrect but Statement II is correct","Both Statement I and Statement II are incorrect"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["2"],"explanation":"Statement I is correct. Ordinary reflection from any surface always transmits or loses some light, so the reflected ray is weaker than the incident ray. TIR reflects essentially all of the light, so the image from the prism is brighter.\nStatement II is incorrect. NCERT clearly states that in total internal reflection no transmission of light takes place.\nAnswer: (2)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Physics, Ch 9 · Ray Optics and Optical Instruments","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT12-PHY-RAY-OPTICS-150","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Ray Optics","qno":150,"subject":"Physics","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Ray Optics","type":"mcq","question":"Assertion (A): For a given pair of media and light of a given colour, the ratio $\\frac{\\sin i}{\\sin r}$ is the same whether the ray strikes the interface at $30^\\circ$ or at $50^\\circ$.\nReason (R): The relative refractive index $n_{21}$ of a pair of media depends on the wavelength of light.","image":"","options":["Both A and R are true and R is the correct explanation of A","Both A and R are true but R is not the correct explanation of A","A is true but R is false","A is false but R is true"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["2"],"explanation":"A is true: by Snell's law, $\\frac{\\sin i}{\\sin r} = n_{21}$, a constant for the pair of media. It is independent of the angle of incidence.\nR is true: NCERT notes that $n_{21}$ also depends on the wavelength of light.\nHowever, R does not explain why the ratio is independent of the angle. It describes a different dependence.\nAnswer: (2)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Physics, Ch 9 · Ray Optics and Optical Instruments","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT12-PHY-RAY-OPTICS-151","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Ray Optics","qno":151,"subject":"Physics","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Ray Optics","type":"mcq","question":"A student uses a convex lens of focal length 10 cm as a simple magnifier. She adjusts it so that the virtual image forms at the near point, D = 25 cm. What is the magnification produced?","image":"","options":["2.5","3.5","3.0","4.5"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["2"],"explanation":"Given: f = 10 cm, D = 25 cm, and the image is at the near point.\nFormula: $m = 1 + \\frac{D}{f}$ when the image is at the near point.\nSubstituting: $m = 1 + \\frac{25}{10} = 3.5$.\nThe value 2.5 is $\\frac{D}{f}$. That formula applies only when the image is at infinity.\nAnswer: (2)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Physics, Ch 9 · Ray Optics and Optical Instruments","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT12-PHY-RAY-OPTICS-152","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Ray Optics","qno":152,"subject":"Physics","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Ray Optics","type":"mcq","question":"Statement I: Turpentine has a lower mass density than water but a higher optical density than water.\nStatement II: A ray of light passing obliquely from water into turpentine bends away from the normal.","image":"","options":["Both Statement I and Statement II are correct","Both Statement I and Statement II are incorrect","Statement I is correct but Statement II is incorrect","Statement I is incorrect but Statement II is correct"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["3"],"explanation":"Statement I: Correct. NCERT gives turpentine as an example where optical density and mass density do not go together. Turpentine is lighter than water but optically denser.\nStatement II: Incorrect. Light going from a rarer medium (water) into a denser one (turpentine) has $n_{21} > 1$, so $r < i$. The ray therefore bends towards the normal, not away from it.\nAnswer: (3)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Physics, Ch 9 · Ray Optics and Optical Instruments","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT12-PHY-RAY-OPTICS-153","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Ray Optics","qno":153,"subject":"Physics","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Ray Optics","type":"mcq","question":"How many of the following statements are correct?\n(i) For a simple microscope, the magnification with the image at the near point exceeds that with the image at infinity by exactly 1.\n(ii) In a compound microscope, the final image is erect with respect to the object.\n(iii) The objective of an astronomical refracting telescope has a larger focal length and a larger aperture than its eyepiece.\n(iv) A concave mirror used as a telescope objective suffers no chromatic aberration.\n(v) The resolving power of a telescope does not depend on the diameter of its objective.","image":"","options":["2","3","4","5"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["2"],"explanation":"(i) Correct: the two values are $1+\\frac{D}{f}$ and $\\frac{D}{f}$, which differ by 1.\n(ii) Incorrect: the objective forms an inverted image, so the final image is inverted.\n(iii) Correct: NCERT states the objective has a large focal length and a much larger aperture.\n(iv) Correct: reflection does not depend on wavelength, so a mirror has no chromatic aberration.\n(v) Incorrect: resolving power depends on the objective's diameter.\nThree statements are correct.\nAnswer: (2)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Physics, Ch 9 · Ray Optics and Optical Instruments","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT12-PHY-RAY-OPTICS-154","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Ray Optics","qno":154,"subject":"Physics","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Ray Optics","type":"mcq","question":"A thin convex lens of focal length 20 cm is placed in contact with a thin concave lens of focal length 30 cm, on a common principal axis. An object is placed 90 cm from the combination. How far from the combination is the image formed?","image":"","options":["36 cm","60 cm","90 cm","180 cm"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["4"],"explanation":"Given: $f_1 = +20$ cm, $f_2 = -30$ cm, $u = -90$ cm.\nFormula: $\\frac{1}{f} = \\frac{1}{f_1} + \\frac{1}{f_2}$ and $\\frac{1}{v} - \\frac{1}{u} = \\frac{1}{f}$.\nSubstituting: $\\frac{1}{f} = \\frac{1}{20} - \\frac{1}{30} = \\frac{1}{60}$, so $f = +60$ cm. The combination is converging.\nNext, $\\frac{1}{v} = \\frac{1}{60} - \\frac{1}{90} = \\frac{1}{180}$.\nSo $v = +180$ cm. The image is real and on the far side, with $m = v/u = -2$.\nAnswer: (4)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Physics, Ch 9 · Ray Optics and Optical Instruments","difficulty":"hard"},{"id":"NCERT12-PHY-RAY-OPTICS-155","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Ray Optics","qno":155,"subject":"Physics","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Ray Optics","type":"mcq","question":"A convex lens of focal length 25 cm is placed in contact with a concave lens of focal length 40 cm. What is the power of the combination?","image":"","options":["+1.5 D","+6.5 D","-1.5 D","+0.15 D"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"Given: $f_1 = +25$ cm $= +0.25$ m (convex), $f_2 = -40$ cm $= -0.40$ m (concave).\nFormula: $P = P_1 + P_2$, with $P = \\frac{1}{f}$ and f in metres.\nSubstituting: $P_1 = +4$ D and $P_2 = -2.5$ D, so $P = 4 - 2.5$.\nSo P = +1.5 D. The combination is converging.\nOption (2) wrongly adds the magnitudes of the two powers.\nOption (4) uses f in cm instead of metres.\nAnswer: (1)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Physics, Ch 9 · Ray Optics and Optical Instruments","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT12-PHY-RAY-OPTICS-156","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Ray Optics","qno":156,"subject":"Physics","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Ray Optics","type":"mcq","question":"Statement I: A thin glass lens ($n = 1.5$) of focal length $+20$ cm in air has a focal length of about $+78$ cm when immersed in water ($n = 1.33$).\nStatement II: A convex lens of power $+2.5$ D in contact with a concave lens of power $-4.0$ D behaves as a diverging lens of focal length about $-66.7$ cm.","image":"","options":["Both Statement I and Statement II are true","Both Statement I and Statement II are false","Statement I is true but Statement II is false","Statement I is false but Statement II is true"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"Statement I: Use the lens maker's formula in each medium.\nIn air: $1/20 = 0.5\\,K$, where $K = 1/R_1 - 1/R_2$.\nIn water: $1.33/f = 0.17\\,K$.\nDividing gives $f = 20 \\times 0.5 \\times 1.33/0.17 \\approx 78.2$ cm. True.\nStatement II: $P = 2.5 - 4.0 = -1.5$ D, so $f = 1/(-1.5)$ m $\\approx -66.7$ cm. Negative power means diverging. True.\nAnswer: (1)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Physics, Ch 9 · Ray Optics and Optical Instruments","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT12-PHY-RAY-OPTICS-157","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Ray Optics","qno":157,"subject":"Physics","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Ray Optics","type":"mcq","question":"A prism of refractive index $\\sqrt{3}$, placed in air, has an angle of minimum deviation equal to its own refracting angle $A$. What is the value of $A$?","image":"","options":["$60^\\circ$","$30^\\circ$","$45^\\circ$","$90^\\circ$"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"Given: $D_m = A$ and $n = \\sqrt{3}$.\nFormula: $n = \\frac{\\sin[(A + D_m)/2]}{\\sin(A/2)} = \\frac{\\sin A}{\\sin(A/2)}$.\nSimplifying: $\\frac{2\\sin(A/2)\\cos(A/2)}{\\sin(A/2)} = 2\\cos\\frac{A}{2}$.\nSo $2\\cos\\frac{A}{2} = \\sqrt{3}$, which gives $\\frac{A}{2} = 30^\\circ$.\nSo $A = 60^\\circ$.\nAnswer: (1)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Physics, Ch 9 · Ray Optics and Optical Instruments","difficulty":"hard"},{"id":"NCERT12-PHY-RAY-OPTICS-158","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Ray Optics","qno":158,"subject":"Physics","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Ray Optics","type":"mcq","question":"How many of the following statements are correct?\n(a) If a ray bends towards the normal on entering medium 2 from medium 1, then $n_{21} > 1$.\n(b) A medium of greater mass density is always optically denser.\n(c) The incident ray, the refracted ray and the normal at the point of incidence lie in the same plane.\n(d) A ray incident normally on a plane interface passes into the second medium without bending.\n(e) $n_{12} \\times n_{21} = 1$\n(f) A ray going obliquely from a denser to a rarer medium bends towards the normal.","image":"","options":["2","3","4","5"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["3"],"explanation":"(a) Correct: $r < i$ means $\\frac{\\sin i}{\\sin r} > 1$.\n(b) Incorrect: turpentine is optically denser than water but has a lower mass density.\n(c) Correct: this is the first law of refraction.\n(d) Correct: $i = 0$ gives $\\sin r = 0$, so $r = 0$.\n(e) Correct: $n_{12} = \\frac{1}{n_{21}}$.\n(f) Incorrect: such a ray bends away from the normal.\nSo 4 statements are correct.\nAnswer: (3)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Physics, Ch 9 · Ray Optics and Optical Instruments","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT12-PHY-RAY-OPTICS-159","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Ray Optics","qno":159,"subject":"Physics","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Ray Optics","type":"mcq","question":"A thin lens of power +5 D is placed in contact with a thin lens of power −3 D. What is the focal length of the combination?","image":"","options":["+50 cm","+12.5 cm","−50 cm","+200 cm"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"Given: $P_1$ = +5 D and $P_2$ = −3 D, with the lenses in contact.\nFormula: $P = P_1 + P_2$ (an algebraic sum), and $f = \\frac{1}{P}$.\nSubstituting: P = 5 − 3 = +2 D, so f = 1/2 m = 0.5 m.\nSo f = +50 cm, and the combination is converging.\nAnswer: (1)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Physics, Ch 9 · Ray Optics and Optical Instruments","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT12-PHY-RAY-OPTICS-160","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Ray Optics","qno":160,"subject":"Physics","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Ray Optics","type":"mcq","question":"Assertion (A): A convex lens of focal length 40 cm placed in contact with a concave lens of focal length 25 cm behaves as a diverging lens of power −1.5 D.\nReason (R): The net power of thin lenses in contact is the algebraic sum of their individual powers.","image":"","options":["Both A and R are true and R is the correct explanation of A","Both A and R are true but R is not the correct explanation of A","A is true but R is false","A is false but R is true"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"R is true: for thin lenses in contact, $P = P_1 + P_2 + \\ldots$, taken with signs.\nChecking A: $P_1 = \\frac{1}{0.40}$ = +2.5 D and $P_2 = \\frac{1}{-0.25}$ = −4 D.\nSo P = 2.5 − 4 = −1.5 D. The net power is negative, so the combination diverges. A is true.\nA follows directly from R, so R correctly explains A.\nAnswer: (1)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Physics, Ch 9 · Ray Optics and Optical Instruments","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT12-PHY-RAY-OPTICS-161","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Ray Optics","qno":161,"subject":"Physics","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Ray Optics","type":"mcq","question":"A ray of light in air falls obliquely on one face of a rectangular glass slab and comes out from the opposite parallel face into air. The emergent ray is","image":"","options":["parallel to the incident ray but laterally displaced","along the same straight line as the incident ray","deviated towards the base of the slab","perpendicular to the face of the slab"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"At the first face the ray refracts from air into glass. At the second face it refracts from glass back into air.\nBy Snell's law at both parallel faces, the angle of emergence equals the angle of incidence ($r_2 = i_1$). The emergent ray is therefore parallel to the incident ray, so there is no deviation.\nThe ray has, however, been displaced sideways inside the glass. Option (2) ignores this lateral shift.\nAnswer: (1)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Physics, Ch 9 · Ray Optics and Optical Instruments","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT12-PHY-RAY-OPTICS-162","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Ray Optics","qno":162,"subject":"Physics","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Ray Optics","type":"mcq","question":"How many of the following statements are correct?\n(i) In the Cartesian sign convention, the focal length of a concave mirror is negative.\n(ii) A convex mirror always forms a virtual, diminished image of a real object, located between its pole and focus.\n(iii) For a simple microscope, the angular magnification with the image at infinity is one more than that with the image at the near point.\n(iv) An astronomical telescope in normal adjustment has magnifying power $\\frac{f_o}{f_e}$ and tube length $f_o + f_e$.\n(v) The refractive index $n_{21}$ of a pair of media depends on the angle of incidence.\n(vi) A real image formed by a convex lens exists in space even if no screen is placed there.","image":"","options":["2","3","4","5"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["3"],"explanation":"(i) Correct: the focus lies opposite to the incident light, so $f < 0$.\n(ii) Correct: for any real object, the mirror equation gives $v > 0$ and $0 < v < f$, with $m < 1$.\n(iii) Incorrect: $D/f$ is one less than $1 + D/f$.\n(iv) Correct.\n(v) Incorrect: $n_{21}$ depends on the media and the wavelength, not on $i$.\n(vi) Correct: rays actually converge there, and the screen only diffuses them.\nSo 4 statements are correct.\nAnswer: (3)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Physics, Ch 9 · Ray Optics and Optical Instruments","difficulty":"hard"},{"id":"NCERT12-PHY-RAY-OPTICS-163","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Ray Optics","qno":163,"subject":"Physics","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Ray Optics","type":"mcq","question":"A ray of light in air falls obliquely on one face of a rectangular glass slab and comes out from the opposite parallel face into air. How many of the following statements are correct?\n(i) The emergent ray is parallel to the incident ray.\n(ii) The angle of emergence equals the angle of incidence at the first face.\n(iii) The emergent ray lies along the same straight line as the incident ray, i.e., there is no lateral shift.\n(iv) Inside the slab, the ray bends towards the normal at the first face.","image":"","options":["1","2","3","4"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["3"],"explanation":"(i) Correct: refraction at the two parallel faces cancels the deviation, so the emergent ray is parallel to the incident ray.\n(ii) Correct: from Snell's law at both faces, $r_2 = i_1$.\n(iii) Incorrect: the emergent ray is parallel to the incident ray but displaced sideways. This is the lateral shift.\n(iv) Correct: light passing from air into glass enters a denser medium, so it bends towards the normal.\nThree statements are correct.\nAnswer: (3)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Physics, Ch 9 · Ray Optics and Optical Instruments","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT12-PHY-RAY-OPTICS-164","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Ray Optics","qno":164,"subject":"Physics","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Ray Optics","type":"mcq","question":"A small bulb is placed 40 cm below the surface of a transparent liquid in a wide tank. Viewed from above, light emerges only through a circular patch of the surface of radius 30 cm centred directly above the bulb. Treat the bulb as a point source. What is the speed of light in the liquid? (Take $c = 3 \\times 10^8$ m/s.)","image":"","options":["$1.80 \\times 10^8$ m/s","$2.25 \\times 10^8$ m/s","$2.40 \\times 10^8$ m/s","$1.67 \\times 10^8$ m/s"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"Given: depth $h = 40$ cm and patch radius $r = 30$ cm.\nThe edge of the patch is where rays meet the surface at exactly the critical angle, so $\\tan i_c = \\frac{r}{h} = \\frac{30}{40} = 0.75$.\nFrom this, $\\sin i_c = \\frac{30}{\\sqrt{30^2 + 40^2}} = \\frac{30}{50} = 0.6$.\nFormula: $n = \\frac{1}{\\sin i_c} = \\frac{5}{3}$.\nSubstituting: $v = \\frac{c}{n} = \\frac{3 \\times 10^8}{5/3} = 1.8 \\times 10^8$ m/s.\nUsing 0.75 as if it were $\\sin i_c$ would wrongly give $2.25 \\times 10^8$ m/s.\nAnswer: (1)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Physics, Ch 9 · Ray Optics and Optical Instruments","difficulty":"hard"},{"id":"NCERT12-PHY-RAY-OPTICS-165","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Ray Optics","qno":165,"subject":"Physics","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Ray Optics","type":"mcq","question":"How many of the following statements are correct?\n(i) In total internal reflection, no light is transmitted, so the reflected ray carries essentially all the incident intensity.\n(ii) The critical angle of diamond with respect to air is larger than that of crown glass.\n(iii) A prism that bends light through $90^\\circ$ by total internal reflection needs a material with critical angle less than $45^\\circ$, and crown glass meets this requirement.\n(iv) In the laser demonstration, a few drops of milk are added to the water so that the path of the beam inside the water becomes visible.\n(v) Total internal reflection can occur when light passes from air into water at a large enough angle of incidence.","image":"","options":["1","2","3","4"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["3"],"explanation":"(i) Correct: total internal reflection involves no transmission, unlike ordinary reflection.\n(ii) Incorrect: diamond has $n = 2.42$ and $i_c \\approx 24.4^\\circ$, which is smaller than crown glass at $41.14^\\circ$. A larger $n$ gives a smaller $i_c$.\n(iii) Correct: the ray meets the hypotenuse face at $45^\\circ$, and $41.14^\\circ < 45^\\circ$.\n(iv) Correct: the turbid water scatters light, so the beam's path shines brightly.\n(v) Incorrect: total internal reflection requires travel from a denser to a rarer medium.\nSo 3 statements are correct.\nAnswer: (3)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Physics, Ch 9 · Ray Optics and Optical Instruments","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT12-PHY-RAY-OPTICS-166","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Ray Optics","qno":166,"subject":"Physics","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Ray Optics","type":"mcq","question":"How many of the following are based on total internal reflection?\nOptical fibre used to view the inside of the stomach\nPrism that bends a ray through $90^\\circ$\nSparkling of a diamond\nMirage\nLateral shift of a ray passing through a glass slab\nConvex secondary mirror in a Cassegrain telescope","image":"","options":["2","3","4","5"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["3"],"explanation":"Optical fibre: uses TIR, with repeated reflections at the core–cladding boundary.\nPrism bending light by $90^\\circ$: uses TIR, since $i_c < 45^\\circ$ for glass.\nDiamond: uses TIR. Its small critical angle ($\\approx 24.4^\\circ$) causes multiple internal reflections.\nMirage: listed by NCERT as an example of TIR.\nLateral shift in a slab: due to refraction only, not TIR.\nCassegrain secondary mirror: ordinary reflection from a mirror, not TIR.\nThat gives 4.\nAnswer: (3)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Physics, Ch 9 · Ray Optics and Optical Instruments","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT12-PHY-RAY-OPTICS-167","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Ray Optics","qno":167,"subject":"Physics","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Ray Optics","type":"mcq","question":"Match List-I (telescope) with List-II (feature described in NCERT).\nList-I:\n(A) Yerkes Observatory telescope\n(B) Mt. Palomar telescope\n(C) Keck telescopes\n(D) Kavalur telescope\nList-II:\n(I) Pair of reflectors of 10 m diameter in Hawaii\n(II) Largest lens objective in use, 40 inch diameter\n(III) 2.34 m Cassegrain reflector\n(IV) 200 inch reflector; observer sits in a cage near the focus","image":"","options":["A-II, B-IV, C-I, D-III","A-IV, B-II, C-I, D-III","A-II, B-IV, C-III, D-I","A-III, B-I, C-IV, D-II"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"(A) Yerkes, USA: the largest lens objective in use, 40 inch (about 1.02 m), so A-II.\n(B) Mt. Palomar, California: a 200 inch reflector in which the viewer sits in a small cage near the focal point, so B-IV.\n(C) Keck, Hawaii: a pair of reflectors of 10 m diameter, so C-I.\n(D) Kavalur, Tamil Nadu: a 2.34 m Cassegrain reflector, so D-III.\nAnswer: (1)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Physics, Ch 9 · Ray Optics and Optical Instruments","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT12-PHY-RAY-OPTICS-168","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Ray Optics","qno":168,"subject":"Physics","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Ray Optics","type":"mcq","question":"A small lamp and a screen are fixed 1.6 m apart. A convex lens is to be placed between them so that a sharp image of the lamp forms on the screen. What is the maximum focal length the lens can have?","image":"","options":["40 cm","80 cm","160 cm","20 cm"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"Given: object–screen distance D = 160 cm.\nCondition: for a real image, u + v = D. Combined with the lens formula, this gives a quadratic in u that has real roots only if D ≥ 4f.\nSo the maximum focal length is f = D/4.\nSubstituting: f = 160/4 = 40 cm. At this value the lens sits midway, with u = v = 2f = 80 cm.\nAnswer: (1)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Physics, Ch 9 · Ray Optics and Optical Instruments","difficulty":"medium"}]});
